---
Part II, Chapter 6: The multi-file AST
Package: packages/loom-ast/src/ast/LoomCorpusAst.ts
Language: TypeScript
---

# The multi-file AST

`LoomCorpusAst` is the multi-file AST — pure data, no pipeline (that is `LoomCorpusAstBuilder`). Every loaded `.loom` is a `LoomModule` keyed by its path, holding its source, parsed `doc`, its composed `product`, and import edges. The corpus is just a map of those modules. The `product` is what the product pass builds for each module; a cross-file reference stays an edge there, resolved later at projection by `fromProduct`.

## The module and the corpus

A `Location` names a loom, and it is a module's identity. The source that keeps the loom makes it, as the chapter "Where a workspace is kept" describes, and the corpus compares locations and keys its maps by them. On the file system a location is the loom's resolved path. A `LoomModule` carries one file's `product` beside its `text` and parsed `doc`. The `product` is the module's composition graph, built straight from the parse, so every loaded module carries one. A `LoomCorpusAst` is the map of modules by path.

=>

export type Location = string

export const LoomModuleSchema = Schema.Struct({
  path: Schema.String,
  text: Schema.String,
  doc: LoomDocumentSchema,
  product: ProductSchema,
})
export type LoomModule = typeof LoomModuleSchema.Type

export const LoomCorpusAstSchema = Schema.Struct({
  modules: Schema.ReadonlyMap(Schema.String, LoomModuleSchema),
})
export type LoomCorpusAst = typeof LoomCorpusAstSchema.Type

## The module map

A `Modules` map keys each loom in a corpus by its path. The corpus is one map; the graph over it is the anchor graph, where a section in one loom composes a section in another. The forward reach a run and a tangle scope to, and the reverse reach an edit reprojects, both walk that graph, so both live beside it.

=>

type Modules = ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>

## Resolving names

Names resolve here: the lookups that turn an `::[Name]` anchor into the section it reaches. A `SectionRef` locates that section by the module it lives in and the section itself.

A name resolves nearest first, outward from the anchor. A bare `::[Name]` looks first among the sections of its own loom. When none of them carries the name, it looks among the chapters of the anchor's own book, and a chapter it finds stands for the chapter's opening section. A loom's book is the one its `Book:` line names, or the corpus's default book when it names none, as the chapter "Books" describes. A bare name never reaches a chapter of another book. The rule holds wherever the anchor stands, in a section's code as in its prose.

An author names a section of another chapter with a path. A path lists names from the outside in, separated by commas, and each name after the first lies inside the one before it. `::[The multi-file AST, Resolving names]` names a section of this chapter from anywhere in its book, and `::[The shape of a loom, The multi-file AST, Resolving names]` names the same section through its part. A path that starts with a book's name reaches into that book, as `::[The website, Part I, Chapter 2, Pricing]` does from a chapter of another book. A comma splits a name into a path only when the whole name matches nothing, so a title that holds a comma, such as `Once, as the site is built`, still resolves whole.

A name and a path of names are the only ways an anchor reaches a section. Loom reads no loom's path in an anchor, and the product pass faults an anchor that still writes one in parentheses after its name.

`titlesIn` indexes one loom's titled sections by their raw title. The first of any duplicate within the loom wins. An anchor that names a duplicated title gets no such tie-break, because the product pass reports it as ambiguous. `titleIndexes` builds that index for every loom, once.

=>

export type SectionRef = { readonly module: Location; readonly section: LoomSection }

const titlesIn = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyMap<string, SectionRef> =>
  pipe(
    module.doc.sections,
    Array.map((section) =>
      Option.map(
        Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.title),
        (title) => [title.source, { module: module.path, section }] as const,
      ),
    ),
    Array.getSomes,
    Array.reduce(new Map<string, SectionRef>(), (index, [title, ref]) =>
      index.has(title) ? index : new Map(index).set(title, ref),
    ),
  )

export type TitleIndexes = ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyMap<string, SectionRef>>

export const titleIndexes = (modules: Modules): TitleIndexes =>
  new Map(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()).map((m) => [m.path, titlesIn(m)] as const))

~

A lookup finds a chapter by the title the chapter declares, which `chapterTitleToken` reads here and in the navigation below. A `Chapter` pairs that title with the loom that declares it, its own number, the book it belongs to, and the part it belongs to. A chapter states its part in one of three ways. A part's opening chapter announces the part's number and name, as `Part IV: The product` does. Every other chapter of a numbered part gives only the number, as `Part IV, Chapter 2` does. A chapter outside the numbered parts gives the part's name alone, as `Part: Work in progress` does.

`bookOf` reads the book a loom names, and gives nothing for a loom of the default book. `sameBook` tells whether two looms stand in one book, the default book included. A book numbers its parts on its own, so the game's Part I and the website's Part I are two parts, and `partKey` writes a part's number together with its book.

`partNamesIn` maps each part, by that key, to the name its opening chapter announces. `partOf` reads the part a chapter belongs to as a `PartRef`: the chapter's book, the number the chapter gives, and the name it gives or its number maps to. `samePart` tells whether two parts are one. They stand in one book, and share a number when both give one, or a name otherwise.

A book loom opens its book rather than standing in it, as the chapter "Books" describes. `isBookLoom` tells one: its frontmatter names a book and no part, and it declares no title, so it names no chapter or discussion either. `chaptersIn` lists every chapter of the corpus, the book looms aside, so two chapters that share a title both stay in reach. `openingSectionOf` gives a loom's first titled section, the section that stands for its chapter.

=>

type PartRef = {
  readonly book: Option.Option<string>
  readonly number: Option.Option<string>
  readonly name: Option.Option<string>
}

type Chapter = {
  readonly title: string
  readonly number: Option.Option<string>
  readonly discussion: boolean
  readonly book: Option.Option<string>
  readonly part: PartRef
  readonly module: LoomModule
}

export const bookOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.book), (book) => book.value)

const sameBook = (book: Option.Option<string>, other: Option.Option<string>): boolean =>
  Option.getOrNull(book) === Option.getOrNull(other)

const partKey = (book: Option.Option<string>, number: string): string =>
  JSON.stringify([Option.getOrNull(book), number])

const partNamesIn = (modules: Modules): ReadonlyMap<string, string> =>
  new Map(
    Array.getSomes(
      Array.map(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) =>
        pipe(
          Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
          Option.flatMap((fm) =>
            Option.all([Option.fromNullishOr(fm.part), Option.fromNullishOr(fm.partName)]),
          ),
          Option.map(
            ([part, partName]) => [partKey(bookOf(module), part.value), partName.value] as const,
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

const partOf = (names: ReadonlyMap<string, string>, module: LoomModule): PartRef => {
  const book = bookOf(module)
  const frontmatter = Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter)
  const number = pipe(
    frontmatter,
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((fm) => fm.part),
    Option.map((part) => part.value),
  )
  return {
    book,
    number,
    name: Option.orElse(
      pipe(
        frontmatter,
        Option.flatMapNullishOr((fm) => fm.partName),
        Option.map((partName) => partName.value),
      ),
      () => Option.flatMap(number, (n) => Option.fromNullishOr(names.get(partKey(book, n)))),
    ),
  }
}

const samePart = (part: PartRef, other: PartRef): boolean =>
  sameBook(part.book, other.book) &&
  Option.match(Option.all([part.number, other.number]), {
    onSome: ([number, otherNumber]) => number === otherNumber,
    onNone: () => Option.exists(part.name, (name) => Option.contains(other.name, name)),
  })

export const isBookLoom = (module: LoomModule): boolean =>
  Option.exists(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
    (fm) =>
      fm.book !== undefined &&
      fm.title === undefined &&
      fm.part === undefined &&
      fm.partName === undefined,
  )

const chaptersIn = (modules: Modules): ReadonlyArray<Chapter> => {
  const names = partNamesIn(modules)
  return pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(modules.values()),
    Array.filter((module) => !isBookLoom(module)),
    Array.map((module) =>
      Option.map(chapterTitleToken(module), (title) => ({
        title: title.source,
        number: pipe(
          Option.fromNullishOr(
            module.doc.frontmatter?.chapter ?? module.doc.frontmatter?.discussion,
          ),
          Option.map((ordinal) => ordinal.value),
          Option.filter((ordinal) => ordinal !== ''),
        ),
        discussion: module.doc.frontmatter?.discussion !== undefined,
        book: bookOf(module),
        part: partOf(names, module),
        module,
      })),
    ),
    Array.getSomes,
  )
}

const openingSectionOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<LoomSection> =>
  Array.findFirst(module.doc.sections, (section) => section.heading.title !== undefined)

~

A `Catalogue` gathers what a lookup reads: the corpus's looms, each loom's titles, every chapter of every book, and each loom's boxes that point to a chapter or a discussion, which the section "Boxes that point to looms" describes. `catalogueOf` builds it from the modules once, for a caller that resolves many names over the same corpus.

`bookAt` reads the book of the loom an anchor stands in, the book its bare names look through. `isBook` tells whether a name names a book, which it does when some loom of the corpus names that book.

=>

export type Catalogue = {
  readonly modules: Modules
  readonly titles: TitleIndexes
  readonly chapters: ReadonlyArray<Chapter>
  readonly boxes: ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyArray<BoxRef>>
}

export const catalogueOf = (modules: Modules): Catalogue => ({
  modules,
  titles: titleIndexes(modules),
  chapters: chaptersIn(modules),
  boxes: new Map(
    Array.map(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) => [
      module.path,
      pointingBoxesOf(module),
    ]),
  ),
})

const bookAt = (catalogue: Catalogue, from: Location): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.flatMap(Option.fromNullishOr(catalogue.modules.get(from)), bookOf)

const isBook = (catalogue: Catalogue, name: string): boolean =>
  Array.some(Array.fromIterable(catalogue.modules.values()), (module) =>
    Option.contains(bookOf(module), name),
  )

~

A lookup walks the corpus from the outside in, and records where each name led. A `Place` is one stop on the way: a book, a part, a chapter, a section, or a box that points nowhere yet: to a chapter or a discussion nobody has written, or a trope that names no section or chapter, which the section "Boxes that point to looms" describes. A `Segment` is one name of a path, with its offsets inside the anchor's name, so the editor can rewrite that one name in a rename. A `Step` pairs a segment with the place it named, and records whether the segment named it by number. It also records the trope box it passed `through`, when a segment named a trope and the step leads on to the trope's entry point, so a rename of that entry point leaves the trope's name alone. A `Trail` lists the steps of one resolution, from the outermost name to the place the anchor reaches, and a name that resolves whole leaves a trail of one step.

=>

export type Place = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  Book: { readonly book: string }
  Part: { readonly part: PartRef }
  Chapter: { readonly chapter: Chapter }
  Section: { readonly ref: SectionRef }
  Box: { readonly ref: BoxRef }
}>

const Place = Data.taggedEnum<Place>()

export type Segment = { readonly text: string; readonly start: number; readonly end: number }

export type Step = {
  readonly segment: Segment
  readonly place: Place
  readonly numbered: boolean
  readonly through: Option.Option<BoxRef>
}

export type Trail = Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<Step>

~

A name in a path is a title or a number. `Part II` names a part by its number, `Chapter 6` a chapter by its number, and `Discussion 1` a discussion by its number, the way a loom's identity line names them, and any other name is a title. A discussion is a loom of its own kind, which sits in its part beside the chapters, so a title finds a discussion wherever it finds a chapter. Chapters and discussions are numbered apart, so a number finds only its own kind, through `numberedSteps`. `numberIn` reads the number out of a name that opens with a keyword: `Part`, `Chapter`, or `Discussion`.

`stepsNamed` finds the places a segment can mean inside a scope, and records for each whether the segment named it by number. At the top, a segment names a book, or a part or a chapter of the anchor's own book. Inside a book, it names a part or a chapter of that book. Inside a part, it names a chapter of that part. Inside a chapter, it names one of the chapter's sections by its title, since a section has no number, or one of the chapter's boxes that point to a loom, by its name. Inside a section or a box, it finds nothing, so the walk ends there. `partsWhere` finds a part of a book by a test on its `PartRef`, and keeps the reference of the first chapter in it, which carries the part's number and its name both. `chaptersWithin` finds the chapters of a book, or of one part of it, that pass a test.

`walk` follows a path's segments through `stepsNamed`, one scope deeper for each, starting from the anchor's own book. A book and a part each group chapters and stand for no section of their own, which `groupsChapters` tells. So a trail is `finished` anywhere else, a trail that ends at a book or a part resolves nothing, and a bare name never finds either.

=>

const numberIn = (keyword: string, name: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.fromNullishOr(new RegExp(`^${keyword}\\s+(\\S+)$`).exec(name)?.[1])

const chaptersWithin = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  book: Option.Option<string>,
  part: Option.Option<PartRef>,
  test: (chapter: Chapter) => boolean,
): ReadonlyArray<Chapter> =>
  Array.filter(
    catalogue.chapters,
    (chapter) =>
      test(chapter) &&
      sameBook(chapter.book, book) &&
      Option.match(part, {
        onNone: () => true,
        onSome: (part) => samePart(chapter.part, part),
      }),
  )

const partsWhere = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  book: Option.Option<string>,
  test: (part: PartRef) => boolean,
): ReadonlyArray<PartRef> =>
  Option.toArray(
    Option.map(
      Array.findFirst(
        catalogue.chapters,
        (chapter) => sameBook(chapter.book, book) && test(chapter.part),
      ),
      (chapter) => chapter.part,
    ),
  )

const sectionTitled = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: Location,
  title: string,
): Option.Option<SectionRef> => Option.fromNullishOr(catalogue.titles.get(module)?.get(title))

const numberedSteps = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  book: Option.Option<string>,
  part: Option.Option<PartRef>,
  keyword: string,
  discussion: boolean,
  step: (place: Place) => Step,
  segment: Segment,
): ReadonlyArray<Step> =>
  Option.match(numberIn(keyword, segment.text), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Step> => [],
    onSome: (number) =>
      Array.map(
        chaptersWithin(
          catalogue,
          book,
          part,
          (chapter) => chapter.discussion === discussion && Option.contains(chapter.number, number),
        ),
        (chapter) => step(Place.Chapter({ chapter })),
      ),
  })

const stepsNamed = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  own: Option.Option<string>,
  scope: Option.Option<Place>,
  segment: Segment,
): ReadonlyArray<Step> => {
  const step =
    (numbered: boolean) =>
    (place: Place): Step => ({ segment, place, numbered, through: Option.none() })
  const chapterSteps = (
    book: Option.Option<string>,
    part: Option.Option<PartRef>,
  ): ReadonlyArray<Step> => [
    ...Array.map(
      chaptersWithin(catalogue, book, part, (chapter) => chapter.title === segment.text),
      (chapter) => step(false)(Place.Chapter({ chapter })),
    ),
    ...numberedSteps(catalogue, book, part, 'Chapter', false, step(true), segment),
    ...numberedSteps(catalogue, book, part, 'Discussion', true, step(true), segment),
  ]
  const bookSteps = (book: Option.Option<string>): ReadonlyArray<Step> => [
    ...Array.map(
      partsWhere(catalogue, book, (part) => Option.contains(part.name, segment.text)),
      (part) => step(false)(Place.Part({ part })),
    ),
    ...Option.match(numberIn('Part', segment.text), {
      onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Step> => [],
      onSome: (number) =>
        Array.map(
          partsWhere(catalogue, book, (part) => Option.contains(part.number, number)),
          (part) => step(true)(Place.Part({ part })),
        ),
    }),
    ...chapterSteps(book, Option.none()),
  ]
  return Option.match(scope, {
    onNone: () => [
      ...(isBook(catalogue, segment.text) ? [step(false)(Place.Book({ book: segment.text }))] : []),
      ...bookSteps(own),
    ],
    onSome: (place) =>
      Match.value(place).pipe(
        Match.tag('Book', ({ book }) => bookSteps(Option.some(book))),
        Match.tag('Part', ({ part }) => chapterSteps(part.book, Option.some(part))),
        Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) =>
          Option.toArray(
            Option.orElse(
              Option.map(sectionTitled(catalogue, chapter.module.path, segment.text), (ref) =>
                step(false)(Place.Section({ ref })),
              ),
              () =>
                Option.map(boxNamed(catalogue, chapter.module.path, segment.text), (ref) => ({
                  ...step(false)(boxPlace(catalogue, ref)),
                  through: throughOf(ref),
                })),
            ),
          ),
        ),
        Match.tag('Section', (): ReadonlyArray<Step> => []),
        Match.tag('Box', (): ReadonlyArray<Step> => []),
        Match.exhaustive,
      ),
  })
}

const groupsChapters = (place: Place): boolean => place._tag === 'Book' || place._tag === 'Part'

const finished = (trail: ReadonlyArray<Step>): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(trail) && !groupsChapters(Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place)
    ? [trail]
    : []

const walk = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  own: Option.Option<string>,
  trail: ReadonlyArray<Step>,
  segments: ReadonlyArray<Segment>,
): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  Array.matchLeft(segments, {
    onEmpty: () => finished(trail),
    onNonEmpty: (segment, rest) =>
      Array.flatMap(
        stepsNamed(
          catalogue,
          own,
          Option.map(Array.last(trail), (step) => step.place),
          segment,
        ),
        (step) => walk(catalogue, own, Array.append(trail, step), rest),
      ),
  })

~

`trailsOf` puts the four steps in their order. It looks among the sections of the anchor's own loom, then among its boxes that point to a loom, then among the chapters, then along the name split into a path, and `nearest` keeps the first step that finds anything. `chapterTrails` takes the last two steps alone, which look through a book and never in the anchor's own loom. `trailsOf` hands it the book the anchor stands in. `whole` makes a name into one segment that spans all of it, and `segmentsOf` splits a name at its commas, trimming the spaces around each. Every trail `trailsOf` returns lies at the nearest scope where the name matches at all. More than one trail is an ambiguity, which the faults report and the navigation declines to guess through.

`anchorTrails` resolves an anchor token by its name, through `trailsOf`. `sectionOf` reads the section a trail reaches, which is either the section itself or a chapter's opening section. A box whose chapter is not written yet reaches no section. `resolveName` resolves a name to the one section it reaches, and to nothing when no trail or more than one is found. `resolveAnchor` does the same for an anchor token.

=>

const whole = (name: string): Segment => ({ text: name, start: 0, end: name.length })

const segmentsOf = (name: string): ReadonlyArray<Segment> =>
  Array.map(Array.fromIterable(name.matchAll(/[^,]+/g)), (match) => {
    const text = match[0].trim()
    const start = match.index + match[0].length - match[0].trimStart().length
    return { text, start, end: start + text.length }
  })

const sectionTrail = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: Location, name: string): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  Option.toArray(
    Option.map(sectionTitled(catalogue, module, name), (ref): Trail => [
      {
        segment: whole(name),
        place: Place.Section({ ref }),
        numbered: false,
        through: Option.none(),
      },
    ]),
  )

const nothingFound: ReadonlyArray<Trail> = []

const nearest = (lookups: ReadonlyArray<() => ReadonlyArray<Trail>>): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  Array.reduce(lookups, nothingFound, (found, lookup) => (found.length > 0 ? found : lookup()))

const chapterTrails = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  own: Option.Option<string>,
  name: string,
): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  nearest([
    () => walk(catalogue, own, [], [whole(name)]),
    () => walk(catalogue, own, [], segmentsOf(name)),
  ])

export const trailsOf = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  from: Location,
  name: string,
): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  nearest([
    () => sectionTrail(catalogue, from, name),
    () => boxTrail(catalogue, from, name),
    () => chapterTrails(catalogue, bookAt(catalogue, from), name),
  ])

export const anchorTrails = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  from: Location,
  anchor: WarpAnchorToken,
): ReadonlyArray<Trail> => trailsOf(catalogue, from, anchor.name.value)

export const sectionOf = (trail: Trail): Option.Option<SectionRef> =>
  placeSectionOf(Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place)

const placeSectionOf = (place: Place): Option.Option<SectionRef> =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) => Option.some(ref)),
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) =>
      Option.map(openingSectionOf(chapter.module), (section) => ({
        module: chapter.module.path,
        section,
      })),
    ),
    Match.tag('Book', () => Option.none<SectionRef>()),
    Match.tag('Part', () => Option.none<SectionRef>()),
    Match.tag('Box', () => Option.none<SectionRef>()),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const soleTrail = (trails: ReadonlyArray<Trail>): Option.Option<Trail> =>
  trails.length === 1 ? Array.head(trails) : Option.none()

export const resolveName = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  from: Location,
  name: string,
): Option.Option<SectionRef> =>
  Option.flatMap(soleTrail(trailsOf(catalogue, from, name)), sectionOf)

export const resolveAnchor = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  from: Location,
  anchor: WarpAnchorToken,
): Option.Option<SectionRef> =>
  Option.flatMap(soleTrail(anchorTrails(catalogue, from, anchor)), sectionOf)

~

A trope's root lists its entries, the chapters that a chapter outside the trope may reach, in an `Entries:` line, as Part XIII, Tropes, describes. Each name on that line resolves like an anchor's name in the root's loom, by title or by path, but always to a whole chapter. A section of the root's own loom never stands in for one, so an entry reads only the chapter steps of `chapterTrails`, through the root's book. `tropeEntryChapterOf` gives the one chapter an entry's name leads to, and nothing when the name leads nowhere, to several places, or to a place that is not a chapter. `tropeEntryModuleOf` gives the loom of that chapter.

=>

const tropeEntryChapterOf = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  from: Location,
  name: string,
): Option.Option<Chapter> =>
  pipe(
    soleTrail(chapterTrails(catalogue, bookAt(catalogue, from), name)),
    Option.map((trail) => Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place),
    Option.flatMap((place) =>
      place._tag === 'Chapter' ? Option.some(place.chapter) : Option.none(),
    ),
  )

export const tropeEntryModuleOf = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  from: Location,
  name: string,
): Option.Option<LoomModule> =>
  Option.map(tropeEntryChapterOf(catalogue, from, name), (chapter) => chapter.module)

~

An ambiguous name reaches several places, and a longer path can name each of them alone. `pathsTo` gives, for each trail of an ambiguous name, the shortest path that leads from the anchor's loom to that trail's place and to nothing else. The faults offer these paths as fixes.

`namesFor` lists the paths that can name a place, shortest first. A path names a chapter by its title, or by its part and its title. A path names a section by its chapter and its title, or by the chapter's part, the chapter, and the title, and it names a box the same way, by the box's name. `chapterOf` finds the chapter a section's loom declares, and `throughPart` adds the longer path when the chapter belongs to a part. `throughBook` adds a path that starts with the chapter's book for each of those, when the chapter belongs to a named book, so an anchor in another book reaches it too. `samePlace` compares two places, and `leadsTo` resolves a path through `trailsOf` and accepts it when its one trail ends at the place. A place that no path names alone gets none, such as one of two sections that share a title in one loom.

=>

const chapterOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: Location): Option.Option<Chapter> =>
  Array.findFirst(catalogue.chapters, (chapter) => chapter.module.path === module)

const throughPart = (
  chapter: Chapter,
  names: ReadonlyArray<string>,
): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => [
  names,
  ...Option.toArray(Option.map(chapter.part.name, (part) => [part, ...names])),
]

const throughBook = (
  chapter: Chapter,
  paths: ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>>,
): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => [
  ...paths,
  ...Option.match(chapter.book, {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => [],
    onSome: (book) => Array.map(paths, (path) => [book, ...path]),
  }),
]

const namesFor = (catalogue: Catalogue, place: Place): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) =>
      throughBook(chapter, throughPart(chapter, [chapter.title])),
    ),
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) =>
      Option.match(
        Option.all([
          chapterOf(catalogue, ref.module),
          Option.fromNullishOr(ref.section.heading.title),
        ]),
        {
          onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => [],
          onSome: ([chapter, title]) =>
            throughBook(chapter, throughPart(chapter, [chapter.title, title.source])),
        },
      ),
    ),
    Match.tag('Box', ({ ref }) =>
      Option.match(chapterOf(catalogue, ref.module), {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => [],
        onSome: (chapter) =>
          throughBook(chapter, throughPart(chapter, [chapter.title, ref.name.value])),
      }),
    ),
    Match.tag('Book', (): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => []),
    Match.tag('Part', (): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => []),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const samePlace = (place: Place, other: Place): boolean =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Book', ({ book }) => other._tag === 'Book' && other.book === book),
    Match.tag('Part', ({ part }) => other._tag === 'Part' && samePart(part, other.part)),
    Match.tag(
      'Chapter',
      ({ chapter }) =>
        other._tag === 'Chapter' && other.chapter.module.path === chapter.module.path,
    ),
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) => namesSection(ref)(other)),
    Match.tag('Box', ({ ref }) => namesBox(ref)(other)),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const leadsTo =
  (catalogue: Catalogue, from: Location, place: Place) =>
  (path: string): boolean =>
    Option.exists(soleTrail(trailsOf(catalogue, from, path)), (trail) =>
      samePlace(Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place, place),
    )

export const pathsTo = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  from: Location,
  trails: ReadonlyArray<Trail>,
): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  Array.getSomes(
    Array.map(trails, (trail) => {
      const place = Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place
      return Array.findFirst(
        Array.map(namesFor(catalogue, place), (names) => Array.join(names, ', ')),
        leadsTo(catalogue, from, place),
      )
    }),
  )

~

`anchorsOf` gathers every anchor a section carries — across its preamble and its body, code and prose alike — since the reachability walk and the navigation read the same set.

=>

const anchorsOf = (section: LoomSection): ReadonlyArray<WarpAnchorToken> =>
  pipe(
    [...section.preamble, ...section.code],
    Array.flatMap((weft) => weft.anchors),
  )

## Boxes that point to looms

A box of the `Chapter` kind points to a chapter, the one its name titles, and a box of the `Discussion` kind points to a discussion in the same way, as the chapter "The leaf tokens" describes. The box stands where a thought leaves its section for a loom of its own, and it often comes before that loom does. So a name that finds the box finds the loom once it is written, and finds the box itself until then.

A box of the `Trope` kind names a trope, a concept whose size the author has not settled, as Part XIII, Tropes, describes. Its name is the trope's name, and its `anchors` key points to the section or chapter that holds the trope, its entry point. A name that finds a trope box finds the entry point once `anchors` names one, and finds the box itself until then. Moving the trope from a section to a chapter of its own changes only the `anchors` value, so no anchor that names the trope changes.

An `OpenedBox` holds the lines of a loom that make up one box: the `opener`, the line that opens the box, with its `box` token, the box's `body`, and its `closer`, the line that closes it, when one does. `linesOf` lists a document's lines in the order they are written, its preamble and then each section's two runs. `boxFrom` reads one box from the head of a run of lines, and `boxesAmong` reads every box in a run, with `Array.chop` passing over each line outside a box. `openedBoxesOf` reads the boxes of a whole document.

A box's name is also its caption, which the editor and the woven page show with it. `captionOf` reads it, and a box with no name takes its kind for a caption, so a folded note still says what it is.

A `BoxRef` is an opened box that holds a place in the book: one of a kind that points somewhere, with a name, its kind, and the location of its own loom. A trope box also carries the value of its `anchors` key and the values of its `provides` key, which `parameterValues` reads from the box's braces. `pointingBoxesOf` reads every such box from a loom. The `Catalogue` holds them by loom, which `boxesIn` reads.

=>

export type OpenedBox = {
  readonly box: BoxToken
  readonly opener: LoomWeft
  readonly body: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>
  readonly closer: Option.Option<LoomWeft>
}

export type BoxRef = OpenedBox & {
  readonly module: Location
  readonly name: BoxNameToken
  readonly kind: PointingKind
  readonly anchors: Option.Option<ParameterValueToken>
  readonly provides: ReadonlyArray<ParameterValueToken>
}

const linesOf = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> => [
  ...doc.preamble,
  ...Array.flatMap(doc.sections, (section) => [...section.preamble, ...section.code]),
]

const closesBox = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  Option.exists(boxOf(weft), (box) => box.side === 'close')

const boxFrom = (
  lines: Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>,
): readonly [Option.Option<OpenedBox>, ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>] => {
  const opener = Array.headNonEmpty(lines)
  const rest = Array.tailNonEmpty(lines)
  return Option.match(
    Option.filter(boxOf(opener), (box) => box.side === 'open'),
    {
      onNone: () => [Option.none(), rest],
      onSome: (box) => {
        const end = Option.getOrElse(Array.findFirstIndex(rest, closesBox), () => rest.length)
        return [
          Option.some({
            box,
            opener,
            body: Array.take(rest, end),
            closer: Array.get(rest, end),
          }),
          Array.drop(rest, end + 1),
        ]
      },
    },
  )
}

const boxesAmong = (lines: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<OpenedBox> =>
  Array.getSomes(Array.chop(lines, boxFrom))

export const openedBoxesOf = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<OpenedBox> =>
  boxesAmong(linesOf(doc))

export const captionOf = (opened: OpenedBox): string =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(opened.box.name), (name) => name.value),
    () => boxKind(opened.box),
  )

const parameterValues = (box: BoxToken, key: string): ReadonlyArray<ParameterValueToken> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(box.specifier),
    Option.flatMap((specifier) =>
      specifier.type === 'Parameters'
        ? Array.findFirst(specifier.parameters, (parameter) => parameter.key.value === key)
        : Option.none(),
    ),
    Option.map((parameter) => parameter.values),
    Option.getOrElse((): ReadonlyArray<ParameterValueToken> => []),
  )

const pointingBoxesOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<BoxRef> =>
  Array.getSomes(
    Array.map(openedBoxesOf(module.doc), (opened) => {
      const kind = boxKind(opened.box)
      return isPointingKind(kind)
        ? Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(opened.box.name), (name): BoxRef => ({
            ...opened,
            module: module.path,
            name,
            kind,
            anchors: Array.head(parameterValues(opened.box, 'anchors')),
            provides: parameterValues(opened.box, 'provides'),
          }))
        : Option.none()
    }),
  )

const boxesIn = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: Location): ReadonlyArray<BoxRef> =>
  Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(catalogue.boxes.get(module)), () => [])

~

`boxTarget` finds the loom a box points to by its name. A chapter box finds a chapter and never a discussion of the same title, and a discussion box finds a discussion and never a chapter. It looks first among the looms of the box's own part, where the editor opens a new one, and then across the box's book. A trope box points by its `anchors` value instead, so `boxTarget` finds no loom for it.

`tropePlaceOf` finds the place a trope box's `anchors` value names: one section or one chapter, found as an anchor's name is found from the box's loom. It looks through the book with its boxes set aside, so a trope never points to itself or through another trope, and a value that names nothing, or more than one place, leaves the trope unsettled.

`boxNamed` finds a box of one loom by its name. `boxPlace` is the place a box stands for in a lookup: its loom or its entry point once there is one, and the box itself until then. `throughOf` records a trope box on the step that passes through it. `boxTrail` is the lookup's step among the boxes of an anchor's own loom, which `trailsOf` takes after the loom's sections and before the book's chapters. So `::[Rooms]` beside a box `::: Rooms {Chapter}` resolves before the chapter exists and after, and `::[Payments]` beside a trope box resolves to whatever its `anchors` value names.

=>

const boxTarget = (catalogue: Catalogue, ref: BoxRef): Option.Option<Chapter> => {
  const titled = (chapter: Chapter): boolean =>
    chapter.discussion === (ref.kind === discussionKind) && chapter.title === ref.name.value
  return ref.kind === tropeKind
    ? Option.none()
    : Option.orElse(
        Option.flatMap(chapterOf(catalogue, ref.module), (own) =>
          Array.head(chaptersWithin(catalogue, own.book, Option.some(own.part), titled)),
        ),
        () =>
          Array.head(
            chaptersWithin(catalogue, bookAt(catalogue, ref.module), Option.none(), titled),
          ),
      )
}

const tropePlaceOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, ref: BoxRef): Option.Option<Place> =>
  pipe(
    ref.anchors,
    Option.flatMap((value) => {
      const trails = trailsOf({ ...catalogue, boxes: new Map() }, ref.module, value.value)
      return trails.length === 1 ? Array.head(trails) : Option.none()
    }),
    Option.map((trail) => Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place),
    Option.filter((place) => place._tag === 'Section' || place._tag === 'Chapter'),
  )

const boxNamed = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: Location, name: string): Option.Option<BoxRef> =>
  Array.findFirst(boxesIn(catalogue, module), (ref) => ref.name.value === name)

const boxPlace = (catalogue: Catalogue, ref: BoxRef): Place =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    ref.kind === tropeKind
      ? tropePlaceOf(catalogue, ref)
      : Option.map(boxTarget(catalogue, ref), (chapter) => Place.Chapter({ chapter })),
    () => Place.Box({ ref }),
  )

const throughOf = (ref: BoxRef): Option.Option<BoxRef> =>
  ref.kind === tropeKind ? Option.some(ref) : Option.none()

const boxTrail = (catalogue: Catalogue, from: Location, name: string): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  Option.toArray(
    Option.map(boxNamed(catalogue, from, name), (ref): Trail => [
      {
        segment: whole(name),
        place: boxPlace(catalogue, ref),
        numbered: false,
        through: throughOf(ref),
      },
    ]),
  )

~

The navigation reads a box by its name. `boxLocation` is where a box's name stands, and `namesBox` tests whether a place is one box. `boxesPointingTo` finds every chapter box and discussion box that points to one loom, and `boxesNaming` gives where each one's name stands, so a reference to the loom lists the boxes, and a rename of the loom renames them with it.

A trope box promises what its `provides` key lists. While the trope points nowhere, `promisedThrough` gives an anchor that names it the trope's promised names, so the editor serves them in the code's place, as the chapter "Projecting to virtual code" describes. Once the trope has an entry point, `tropeContractsIn` gives each trope box of a loom as a `TropeContract`: the trope's name, its entry point's section, and the promised names, which the editor checks against the code there. `providedOf` reads each promised name with the place the box writes it.

A trope box names its entry point with its `anchors` value rather than its name. `tropeValuesNaming` finds every trope box whose entry point passes a test, and gives where each `anchors` value stands, so a rename of the entry point rewrites the value with it. `valueLocation` gives the words of a value without its quotes. A value written as a path keeps its words, since a rename of one name in it would need more than one edit. `sameBox` tells whether two references are to one box.

=>

const boxLocation = (ref: BoxRef): CorpusLocation => ({
  path: ref.module,
  position: ref.name.position,
})

const namesBox =
  (target: BoxRef) =>
  (place: Place): boolean =>
    place._tag === 'Box' &&
    place.ref.module === target.module &&
    place.ref.name.position.start.offset === target.name.position.start.offset

const sameBox =
  (target: BoxRef) =>
  (other: BoxRef): boolean =>
    other.module === target.module &&
    other.name.position.start.offset === target.name.position.start.offset

const valueLocation = (ref: BoxRef): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    ref.anchors,
    Option.filter((value) => !value.value.includes(',')),
    Option.map((value): CorpusLocation => {
      const quoted = value.source.startsWith('"') ? 1 : 0
      return {
        path: ref.module,
        position: {
          start: shifted(value.position.start, quoted),
          end: shifted(value.position.end, -quoted),
        },
      }
    }),
  )

export const promisedThrough = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  from: Location,
  name: string,
): ReadonlyArray<Provided> =>
  pipe(
    soleTrail(trailsOf(catalogue, from, name)),
    Option.map(Array.lastNonEmpty),
    Option.filter((step) => step.place._tag === 'Box'),
    Option.flatMap((step) => step.through),
    Option.map((ref) => providedOf(ref)),
    Option.getOrElse((): ReadonlyArray<Provided> => []),
  )

export type TropeContract = {
  readonly trope: string
  readonly entry: SectionRef
  readonly provides: ReadonlyArray<Provided>
}

export const tropeContractsIn = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  path: Location,
): ReadonlyArray<TropeContract> =>
  pipe(
    boxesIn(catalogue, path),
    Array.filter((ref) => ref.kind === tropeKind && Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(ref.provides)),
    Array.flatMap((ref) =>
      Option.toArray(
        Option.map(
          Option.flatMap(tropePlaceOf(catalogue, ref), placeSectionOf),
          (entry): TropeContract => ({ trope: ref.name.value, entry, provides: providedOf(ref) }),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

const providedOf = (ref: BoxRef): ReadonlyArray<Provided> =>
  Array.map(ref.provides, (value): Provided => ({ name: value.value, at: value.position }))

const tropeValuesNaming = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  names: (place: Place) => boolean,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Array.flatten(Array.fromIterable(catalogue.boxes.values())),
    Array.filter((ref) => Option.exists(tropePlaceOf(catalogue, ref), names)),
    Array.flatMap((ref) => Option.toArray(valueLocation(ref))),
  )

const boxesPointingTo = (catalogue: Catalogue, chapter: Chapter): ReadonlyArray<BoxRef> =>
  Array.filter(Array.flatten(Array.fromIterable(catalogue.boxes.values())), (ref) =>
    Option.exists(boxTarget(catalogue, ref), (found) => found.module.path === chapter.module.path),
  )

const boxesNaming = (catalogue: Catalogue, chapter: Chapter): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  ...Array.map(boxesPointingTo(catalogue, chapter), boxLocation),
  ...tropeValuesNaming(catalogue, (place) => namesChapter(chapter)(place)),
]

## Reaching through the corpus

A run and a tangle need only part of the corpus: the entry and the sections it composes. `reachable` is the forward reach over the anchor graph — the entry, every module a section in it names through an `::[…]` anchor, and the modules those name in turn. Tangling one file never composes an unrelated loom, and a loom the entry never anchors never fails its build. `transitiveDependents` is the reverse — every module that composes a section in a given file, transitively — so an edit reprojects each file that inlined it.

`dependentsOf` runs the same reverse reach from several files at once. The compiler uses it to find what a batch of changed files touches, such as the dozens a `git checkout` rewrites, and it reads the anchor graph once for the whole batch rather than once for each file. It returns the dependents alone, never the files it started from. `transitiveDependents` is `dependentsOf` started from one file.

`reach` grows a set along one direction of the edges until it adds nothing more, guarding a cycle so the walk ends. `placeModuleOf` reads the module a place stands in, and `moduleOf` the module a trail ends in.

A `Reach` is one edge out of a module: the `target` loom, and the span `at` which the module reaches it. `reachesOf` lists a module's reaches. Each anchor the module carries reaches every module its name leads to: the one it resolves to, or each candidate of an ambiguous name. Each box that points somewhere reaches the loom it points to, and a trope box reaches the loom of its entry point, at the value of its `anchors` key. The boundaries of a trope judge each reach at its span, as Part XIII, Tropes, describes.

`anchorEdges` reads every reach of the corpus once, as an edge from its module to its target. A change to what a box copies then reaches the loom whose box holds the copy, a change to any module an anchor names reaches the anchor's own loom, and a tangle judges an ambiguous name with all of its candidates in view.

=>

export const reach = (
  seeds: ReadonlyArray<Location>,
  step: (module: Location) => ReadonlyArray<Location>,
): ReadonlySet<Location> => {
  const grow = (acc: ReadonlySet<Location>, frontier: ReadonlyArray<Location>): ReadonlySet<Location> => {
    const next = pipe(
      frontier,
      Array.flatMap(step),
      Array.filter((p) => !acc.has(p)),
      Array.dedupe,
    )
    return next.length === 0 ? acc : grow(new Set([...acc, ...next]), next)
  }
  return grow(new Set(seeds), seeds)
}

const placeModuleOf = (place: Place): Option.Option<Location> =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) => Option.some(ref.module)),
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) => Option.some(chapter.module.path)),
    Match.tag('Book', () => Option.none<Location>()),
    Match.tag('Part', () => Option.none<Location>()),
    Match.tag('Box', () => Option.none<Location>()),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const moduleOf = (trail: Trail): Option.Option<Location> =>
  placeModuleOf(Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place)

export type Reach = { readonly target: Location; readonly at: Position }

const boxReachAt = (ref: BoxRef): Position =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(ref.anchors, (value) => value.position),
    () => ref.name.position,
  )

export const reachesOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Reach> => [
  ...pipe(
    module.doc.sections,
    Array.flatMap(anchorsOf),
    Array.flatMap((anchor) =>
      Array.getSomes(
        Array.map(anchorTrails(catalogue, module.path, anchor), (trail) =>
          Option.map(moduleOf(trail), (target): Reach => ({ target, at: anchor.name.position })),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  ),
  ...Array.getSomes(
    Array.map(boxesIn(catalogue, module.path), (ref) =>
      Option.map(placeModuleOf(boxPlace(catalogue, ref)), (target): Reach => ({
        target,
        at: boxReachAt(ref),
      })),
    ),
  ),
]

const anchorEdges = (
  modules: Modules,
): ReadonlyArray<{ readonly owner: Location; readonly target: Location }> => {
  const catalogue = catalogueOf(modules)
  return Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (m) =>
    Array.map(reachesOf(catalogue, m), ({ target }) => ({ owner: m.path, target })),
  )
}

export const reachable = (modules: Modules, entry: Location): ReadonlyArray<Location> => {
  const edges = anchorEdges(modules)
  return Array.fromIterable(
    reach([entry], (module) =>
      Array.getSomes(
        Array.map(edges, (edge) =>
          edge.owner === module ? Option.some(edge.target) : Option.none(),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )
}

export const dependentsOf = (
  modules: Modules,
  entries: ReadonlyArray<Location>,
): ReadonlyArray<Location> => {
  const edges = anchorEdges(modules)
  const seeds = new Set(entries)
  return pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(
      reach(entries, (module) =>
        Array.getSomes(
          Array.map(edges, (edge) =>
            edge.target === module ? Option.some(edge.owner) : Option.none(),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
    Array.filter((path) => !seeds.has(path)),
  )
}

export const transitiveDependents = (modules: Modules, entry: Location): ReadonlyArray<Location> =>
  dependentsOf(modules, [entry])

## Navigation over the corpus

An anchor reaches a section, and a warp anchor reaches the value warp it names — the editor follows both, and `symbolAt` decides which. It reads the kind under a cursor from `LoomSymbol`'s table, so a section anchor resolves a title across the corpus while a warp anchor resolves a name within its own section, each verb dispatching on the same classifier the mappings and colours read. A `CorpusLocation` is where a step lands: the module and the span, in the source's own offsets, which the editor turns into its own coordinates.

=>

export type CorpusLocation = { readonly path: Location; readonly position: Position }

~

Each step starts from the node under a cursor. `anchorAt` finds the anchor whose `::[…]` span covers a source offset. `titledSectionAt` finds the heading whose title covers it, and `sectionRefAt` pairs that section with its module.

=>

const anchorAt = (modules: Modules, path: Location, offset: number): Option.Option<WarpAnchorToken> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((m) =>
      Array.findFirst(
        Array.flatMap(m.doc.sections, anchorsOf),
        (anchor) => anchor.position.start.offset <= offset && offset <= anchor.position.end.offset,
      ),
    ),
  )

const titledSectionAt = (
  modules: Modules,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<LoomSection> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((m) =>
      Array.findFirst(m.doc.sections, (section) => {
        const title = section.heading.title
        return (
          title !== undefined &&
          title.position.start.offset <= offset &&
          offset <= title.position.end.offset
        )
      }),
    ),
  )

const sectionRefAt = (modules: Modules, path: Location, offset: number): Option.Option<SectionRef> =>
  Option.map(titledSectionAt(modules, path, offset), (section) => ({
    module: path,
    section,
  }))

~

A resolved name then locates its declaration and every anchor that names it. `titleLocation` points at a section's heading title, and `chapterLocation` at the title a chapter declares.

An anchor that holds a path names several places, so the navigation reads the one under the cursor. `segmentPosition` turns a segment's offsets inside the anchor's name into a span of the source, moving the name's own start by `shifted`. `stepAt` picks the step whose segment covers the cursor, or the last step when the cursor sits on a bracket. `anchorStepAt` reads the step under a cursor in an anchor. A `Mention` is a place together with whether it was named by number, and `mentionAt` reads the one under a cursor: the section of a heading, or the step of an anchor.

`anchorsNaming` gathers every anchor across the corpus with a step that a predicate accepts, each by the span a caller picks: the whole anchor for a reference, or the one segment for a rename. `stepsOf` gives an anchor's steps, and only an anchor that resolves to one trail has any, since an ambiguous anchor names nothing for certain. `namesSection` accepts one section, and `namesChapter` accepts one chapter. An anchor names a section only through a segment that writes the section's own title. An anchor that reaches a chapter's opening section through the chapter's title names the chapter, not the section.

=>

type SpanOf = (anchor: WarpAnchorToken, segment: Segment) => Position

const titleLocation = (ref: SectionRef): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(ref.section.heading.title), (title): CorpusLocation => ({
    path: ref.module,
    position: title.position,
  }))

const chapterLocation = (chapter: Chapter): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.map(chapterTitleToken(chapter.module), (title): CorpusLocation => ({
    path: chapter.module.path,
    position: title.position,
  }))

const shifted = (point: Point, by: number): Point =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(point.column), {
    onNone: () => ({ ...point, offset: point.offset + by }),
    onSome: (column) => ({ ...point, offset: point.offset + by, column: column + by }),
  })

const segmentPosition: SpanOf = (anchor, segment) => ({
  start: shifted(anchor.name.position.start, segment.start),
  end: shifted(anchor.name.position.start, segment.end),
})

const wholeAnchor: SpanOf = (anchor) => anchor.position

const stepAt = (trail: Trail, anchor: WarpAnchorToken, offset: number): Step =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Array.findFirst(trail, (step) => covers(segmentPosition(anchor, step.segment), offset)),
    () => Array.lastNonEmpty(trail),
  )

const anchorStepAt = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<Step> =>
  Option.flatMap(anchorAt(modules, path, offset), (anchor) =>
    Option.map(soleTrail(anchorTrails(catalogue, path, anchor)), (trail) =>
      stepAt(trail, anchor, offset),
    ),
  )

type Mention = { readonly place: Place; readonly numbered: boolean }

const mentionAt = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<Mention> =>
  Option.orElse(
    Option.map(sectionRefAt(modules, path, offset), (ref): Mention => ({
      place: Place.Section({ ref }),
      numbered: false,
    })),
    () => anchorStepAt(catalogue, modules, path, offset),
  )

const stepsOf = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  from: Location,
  anchor: WarpAnchorToken,
): ReadonlyArray<Step> =>
  Option.match(soleTrail(anchorTrails(catalogue, from, anchor)), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Step> => [],
    onSome: (trail) => trail,
  })

const anchorsNaming = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  names: (step: Step) => boolean,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (m) =>
    pipe(
      m.doc.sections,
      Array.flatMap(anchorsOf),
      Array.flatMap((anchor) =>
        pipe(
          stepsOf(catalogue, m.path, anchor),
          Array.filter(names),
          Array.map((step): CorpusLocation => ({
            path: m.path,
            position: spanOf(anchor, step.segment),
          })),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

const namesSection =
  (target: SectionRef) =>
  (place: Place): boolean =>
    place._tag === 'Section' &&
    place.ref.module === target.module &&
    place.ref.section === target.section

const namesChapter =
  (chapter: Chapter) =>
  (place: Place): boolean =>
    place._tag === 'Chapter' && place.chapter.module.path === chapter.module.path

~

`sectionDefinitionAt` follows an anchor to what the segment under the cursor names: a section's heading, the title a chapter declares, the name of a box that points nowhere yet, or the line where a book loom names its book. `placeLocation` gives where each of those stands. `sectionNames` gathers a section's heading beside every anchor that names it, and beside the `anchors` value of every trope box that points to it. An anchor that reached the section through a trope box named the trope rather than the section, so it stays out, and a rename of the section leaves the trope's name alone. A chapter whose frontmatter declares no title takes its title from its first heading, and that heading then titles the chapter as well as the section. So its names take in the chapter's too: the boxes that point to it, the names on a trope root's `Entries:` line that make it an entry, the `Rooted in:` lines that name it, and the anchors that name the chapter by its title. `chapterTitledBy` finds the chapter a heading titles this way. `placeNames` gathers the names of a mention. A chapter named by its title goes to `chapterNames`, and a chapter named by its number goes to `chapterNumbers`, which the chapter navigation below defines. A book goes to `bookNames`, which the book navigation below defines. So a rename from a title rewrites titles, and a rename from a number renumbers. `placeNamesAt` reads the mention under a cursor and gathers its names. The exported verbs call these once `symbolAt` names the cursor a section.

=>

const sectionDefinitionAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.flatMap(
    anchorStepAt(catalogueOf(corpus.modules), corpus.modules, path, offset),
    ({ place }) => placeLocation(corpus.modules, place),
  )

const placeLocation = (modules: Modules, place: Place): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) => titleLocation(ref)),
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) => chapterLocation(chapter)),
    Match.tag('Box', ({ ref }) => Option.some(boxLocation(ref))),
    Match.tag('Book', ({ book }) => bookLoomLocation(modules, book)),
    Match.tag('Part', () => Option.none<CorpusLocation>()),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const chapterTitledBy = (catalogue: Catalogue, ref: SectionRef): Option.Option<Chapter> =>
  Array.findFirst(
    catalogue.chapters,
    (chapter) =>
      chapter.module.path === ref.module &&
      chapter.module.doc.frontmatter?.title === undefined &&
      Option.exists(openingSectionOf(chapter.module), (opening) => opening === ref.section),
  )

const sectionNames = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  ref: SectionRef,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  ...Option.toArray(titleLocation(ref)),
  ...anchorsNaming(
    catalogue,
    modules,
    (step) => Option.isNone(step.through) && namesSection(ref)(step.place),
    spanOf,
  ),
  ...tropeValuesNaming(catalogue, (place) => namesSection(ref)(place)),
  ...Option.match(chapterTitledBy(catalogue, ref), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
    onSome: (chapter) => [
      ...boxesNaming(catalogue, chapter),
      ...tropeEntriesNaming(catalogue, modules, chapter),
      ...rootedInNaming(catalogue, modules, chapter),
      ...anchorsNaming(
        catalogue,
        modules,
        (step) =>
          !step.numbered && Option.isNone(step.through) && namesChapter(chapter)(step.place),
        spanOf,
      ),
    ],
  }),
]

const placeNames = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  mention: Mention,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Match.value(mention.place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) => sectionNames(catalogue, modules, ref, spanOf)),
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) =>
      mention.numbered
        ? chapterNumbers(catalogue, modules, chapter, spanOf)
        : chapterNames(catalogue, modules, chapter, spanOf),
    ),
    Match.tag('Box', ({ ref }) => [
      boxLocation(ref),
      ...anchorsNaming(catalogue, modules, (step) => namesBox(ref)(step.place), spanOf),
    ]),
    Match.tag('Book', ({ book }) => bookNames(catalogue, modules, book, spanOf)),
    Match.tag('Part', (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => []),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const placeNamesAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => {
  const catalogue = catalogueOf(corpus.modules)
  return Option.match(mentionAt(catalogue, corpus.modules, path, offset), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
    onSome: (mention) => placeNames(catalogue, corpus.modules, mention, spanOf),
  })
}

~

A warp anchor resolves within its section, not across the corpus. `::[ratio]` names the value warp `ratio` its section declares, or the document's when the section declares none — the same scope `symbolsOf` classifies a warp anchor by, so the editor and the classifier agree. `valueWarpsIn` reads a preamble's value warps, the lang warp excepted. `sectionCovering` gives the section an offset sits in, and `warpDefFor` picks the warp a name sees there: the section's own, else the document's, so a section warp shadows the document's. `warpNameAt` reads the name under a cursor, whether a `{{ratio = …}}` definition or a `::[ratio]` anchor. `warpBindingAt` ties them together — the definition and every anchor that binds to it — and `warpDefLocation` and `warpReferences` shape that binding into the definition span and the reference spans a caller picks.

=>

const covers = (position: Position, offset: number): boolean =>
  position.start.offset <= offset && offset <= position.end.offset

const valueWarpsIn = (
  preamble: ReadonlyArray<{ readonly warps: ReadonlyArray<WarpToken> }>,
): ReadonlyArray<WarpToken> =>
  Array.filter(
    Array.flatMap(preamble, (weft) => weft.warps),
    (warp) => warp.name.value !== 'lang',
  )

const allValueWarpsOf = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<WarpToken> => [
  ...valueWarpsIn(doc.preamble),
  ...Array.flatMap(doc.sections, (section) => valueWarpsIn(section.preamble)),
]

const allAnchorsOf = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<WarpAnchorToken> => [
  ...Array.flatMap(doc.preamble, (weft) => weft.anchors),
  ...Array.flatMap(doc.sections, anchorsOf),
]

const sectionCovering = (module: LoomModule, offset: number): Option.Option<LoomSection> =>
  Array.findFirst(module.doc.sections, (section) => covers(section.position, offset))

const warpDefFor = (
  module: LoomModule,
  section: Option.Option<LoomSection>,
  name: string,
): Option.Option<WarpToken> =>
  pipe(
    section,
    Option.flatMap((s) => Array.findFirst(valueWarpsIn(s.preamble), (w) => w.name.value === name)),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      Array.findFirst(valueWarpsIn(module.doc.preamble), (w) => w.name.value === name),
    ),
  )

const warpNameAt = (module: LoomModule, offset: number): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.orElse(
    Option.map(
      Array.findFirst(allValueWarpsOf(module.doc), (w) => covers(w.name.position, offset)),
      (w) => w.name.value,
    ),
    () =>
      Option.map(
        Array.findFirst(allAnchorsOf(module.doc), (a) => covers(a.name.position, offset)),
        (a) => a.name.value,
      ),
  )

const warpBindingAt = (
  module: LoomModule,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<{
  readonly def: WarpToken
  readonly anchors: ReadonlyArray<WarpAnchorToken>
}> =>
  pipe(
    warpNameAt(module, offset),
    Option.flatMap((name) =>
      Option.map(warpDefFor(module, sectionCovering(module, offset), name), (def) => ({
        def,
        anchors: Array.filter(
          allAnchorsOf(module.doc),
          (anchor) =>
            anchor.name.value === name &&
            Option.getOrUndefined(
              warpDefFor(module, sectionCovering(module, anchor.position.start.offset), name),
            ) === def,
        ),
      })),
    ),
  )

const warpDefLocation = (
  module: LoomModule,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.map(warpBindingAt(module, offset), (binding): CorpusLocation => ({
    path,
    position: binding.def.name.position,
  }))

const warpReferences = (
  module: LoomModule,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
  spanOf: (anchor: WarpAnchorToken) => Position,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.match(warpBindingAt(module, offset), {
    onNone: () => [],
    onSome: (binding) => [
      { path, position: binding.def.name.position },
      ...Array.map(binding.anchors, (anchor): CorpusLocation => ({
        path,
        position: spanOf(anchor),
      })),
    ],
  })

~

A chapter's identity is the title it declares, not its heading, because a heading can differ from that title, or be missing. `chapterTitleToken` reads that identity: the frontmatter `title`, or the first heading for a loom that carries no frontmatter. `firstHeadingTitleOf` reads that heading from the loom's opening section.

`chapterAt` reads the chapter under a cursor: the loom a box's name points to, the chapter a name on an `Entries:` line leads to, the trope chapter a `Rooted in:` line names, which `tropeChapterOf` finds by its title, or the chapter whose frontmatter title the cursor sits on. `tropeEntriesOf` reads the names a loom's `Entries:` line lists. `chapterDefinitionAt` follows it to the title the chapter declares.

`boxesNaming` finds every box that points to a chapter, `tropeEntriesNaming` every trope's entry that names it by its bare title, and `rootedInNaming` every `Rooted in:` line that names it, when it is a trope chapter. `chapterNames` gathers the chapter's declared title, those boxes, trope entries and `Rooted in:` lines, and every anchor that names the chapter by its title, so a reference and a rename move the whole name together. `chapterNamesAt` reads the chapter under a cursor and gathers its names.

A box's name leads where its kind says. `boxDefinitionAt` follows the name of a chapter box or a discussion box to its loom, and the name of a trope box to the trope's entry point, which `tropeBoxAt` finds under the cursor. `boxNamesAt` gathers a box's names. A trope's names are its box's name and every anchor that reached the entry point through the box, which `tropeNames` gathers, so a rename of the trope rewrites them and leaves the entry point alone. A chapter box's names are its chapter's.

A chapter's number is a name of its own. `chapterNumbers` gathers the `Chapter N` the chapter's frontmatter declares and every anchor that names the chapter by that number, so renumbering a chapter rewrites each numbered reference in the same edit. `chapterNumbersAt` gathers them for the chapter whose frontmatter number the cursor sits on.

A part resolves the same way, keyed by its book and its number rather than its title. A part is announced once, in its opening chapter's standalone `Part R: name`, whose `partName` is the part's identity. `partOpenerIndex` maps the key of each part, which `partKey` writes from the part's book and its number, to that announcement. The key under a cursor comes from a chapter's `Part R` and the chapter's book, which `frontmatterPartKeyAt` reads for `partKeyAt`. `partDefinitionAt` follows the key to the opener, and `partGroup` gathers the whole part, the announcement and every chapter's `Part R`, which `partGroupAt` reads from the cursor.

A book is named on `Book:` lines. `bookLoomsOf` finds the book looms that open a book, and `bookLoomLocation` the line where the first of them names it, the book's definition. `bookLines` finds every `Book:` line that names a book, the book loom's and those of the looms that stand in the book. `bookNameAt` reads the book a `Book:` line names under a cursor, and `bookDefinitionAt` follows that name to its book loom. `bookNames` gathers the book's `Book:` lines beside every anchor whose path passes through the book, which `namesBook` tells, and `bookNamesAt` reads them from the cursor. So a rename of a book rewrites its name everywhere in one edit.

=>

const firstHeadingTitleOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<HeadingTitleToken> =>
  Option.flatMap(openingSectionOf(module), (section) => Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.title))

const chapterTitleToken = (
  module: LoomModule,
): Option.Option<{ readonly source: string; readonly position: Position }> =>
  Option.orElse(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.title), () =>
    firstHeadingTitleOf(module),
  )

const chapterAt = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<Chapter> =>
  Option.flatMap(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)), (module) =>
    pipe(
      Option.flatMap(
        Array.findFirst(boxesIn(catalogue, path), (ref) => covers(ref.name.position, offset)),
        (ref) => boxTarget(catalogue, ref),
      ),
      Option.orElse(() =>
        Option.flatMap(
          Array.findFirst(tropeEntriesOf(module), (name) => covers(name.position, offset)),
          (name) => tropeEntryChapterOf(catalogue, path, name.value),
        ),
      ),
      Option.orElse(() =>
        pipe(
          Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.rootedIn),
          Option.filter((line) => covers(line.position, offset)),
          Option.flatMap((line) => tropeChapterOf(catalogue, line.value)),
        ),
      ),
      Option.orElse(() =>
        pipe(
          Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.title),
          Option.filter((title) => covers(title.position, offset)),
          Option.flatMap(() => chapterOf(catalogue, path)),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

const tropeEntriesOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<FrontmatterEntryToken> =>
  Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.entries), () => [])

const tropeChapterOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, name: string): Option.Option<Chapter> => {
  const roots = Array.filter(
    catalogue.chapters,
    (chapter) => chapter.module.doc.frontmatter?.trope?.value === name,
  )
  return roots.length === 1 ? Array.head(roots) : Option.none()
}

const chapterDefinitionAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.flatMap(
    chapterAt(catalogueOf(corpus.modules), corpus.modules, path, offset),
    chapterLocation,
  )

const tropeEntriesNaming = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  chapter: Chapter,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) =>
    pipe(
      tropeEntriesOf(module),
      Array.filter(
        (name) =>
          name.value === chapter.title &&
          Option.exists(
            tropeEntryChapterOf(catalogue, module.path, name.value),
            (found) => found.module.path === chapter.module.path,
          ),
      ),
      Array.map((name): CorpusLocation => ({ path: module.path, position: name.position })),
    ),
  )

const rootedInNaming = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  chapter: Chapter,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) =>
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.rootedIn),
      Option.filter((line) =>
        Option.exists(
          tropeChapterOf(catalogue, line.value),
          (found) => found.module.path === chapter.module.path,
        ),
      ),
      Option.map((line): CorpusLocation => ({ path: module.path, position: line.position })),
      Option.toArray,
    ),
  )

const chapterNames = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  chapter: Chapter,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  ...Option.toArray(chapterLocation(chapter)),
  ...boxesNaming(catalogue, chapter),
  ...tropeEntriesNaming(catalogue, modules, chapter),
  ...rootedInNaming(catalogue, modules, chapter),
  ...anchorsNaming(
    catalogue,
    modules,
    (step) => !step.numbered && Option.isNone(step.through) && namesChapter(chapter)(step.place),
    spanOf,
  ),
]

const tropeBoxAt = (catalogue: Catalogue, path: Location, offset: number): Option.Option<BoxRef> =>
  Array.findFirst(
    boxesIn(catalogue, path),
    (ref) => ref.kind === tropeKind && covers(ref.name.position, offset),
  )

const tropeNames = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  ref: BoxRef,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  boxLocation(ref),
  ...anchorsNaming(catalogue, modules, (step) => Option.exists(step.through, sameBox(ref)), spanOf),
]

const boxDefinitionAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> => {
  const catalogue = catalogueOf(corpus.modules)
  return Option.match(tropeBoxAt(catalogue, path, offset), {
    onSome: (ref) =>
      Option.flatMap(tropePlaceOf(catalogue, ref), (place) => placeLocation(corpus.modules, place)),
    onNone: () => chapterDefinitionAt(corpus, path, offset),
  })
}

const boxNamesAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => {
  const catalogue = catalogueOf(corpus.modules)
  return Option.match(tropeBoxAt(catalogue, path, offset), {
    onSome: (ref) => tropeNames(catalogue, corpus.modules, ref, spanOf),
    onNone: () => chapterNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, spanOf),
  })
}

const chapterNamesAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => {
  const catalogue = catalogueOf(corpus.modules)
  return Option.match(chapterAt(catalogue, corpus.modules, path, offset), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
    onSome: (chapter) => chapterNames(catalogue, corpus.modules, chapter, spanOf),
  })
}

const chapterNumbers = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  chapter: Chapter,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  ...Option.toArray(
    Option.map(
      Option.fromNullishOr(chapter.module.doc.frontmatter?.chapter),
      (number): CorpusLocation => ({ path: chapter.module.path, position: number.position }),
    ),
  ),
  ...anchorsNaming(
    catalogue,
    modules,
    (step) => step.numbered && namesChapter(chapter)(step.place),
    spanOf,
  ),
]

const chapterNumbersAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => {
  const catalogue = catalogueOf(corpus.modules)
  return Option.match(chapterOf(catalogue, path), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
    onSome: (chapter) => chapterNumbers(catalogue, corpus.modules, chapter, spanOf),
  })
}

const partOpenerIndex = (modules: Modules): ReadonlyMap<string, CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(modules.values()),
    Array.map((module) =>
      pipe(
        Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
        Option.flatMap((fm) =>
          Option.all({
            part: Option.fromNullishOr(fm.part),
            partName: Option.fromNullishOr(fm.partName),
          }),
        ),
        Option.map(
          ({ part, partName }) =>
            [
              partKey(bookOf(module), part.value),
              { path: module.path, position: partName.position },
            ] as const,
        ),
      ),
    ),
    Array.getSomes,
    Array.reduce(new Map<string, CorpusLocation>(), (index, [key, location]) =>
      index.has(key) ? index : new Map(index).set(key, location),
    ),
  )

const onToken = (token: { readonly position: Position } | undefined, offset: number): boolean =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(token), {
    onNone: () => false,
    onSome: (t) => covers(t.position, offset),
  })

const frontmatterPartKeyAt = (module: LoomModule, offset: number): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
    Option.filter((fm) => onToken(fm.part, offset) || onToken(fm.partName, offset)),
    Option.flatMap((fm) => Option.fromNullishOr(fm.part)),
    Option.map((part) => partKey(bookOf(module), part.value)),
  )

const partKeyAt = (modules: Modules, path: Location, offset: number): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((module) => frontmatterPartKeyAt(module, offset)),
  )

const partDefinitionAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.flatMap(partKeyAt(corpus.modules, path, offset), (key) =>
    Option.fromNullishOr(partOpenerIndex(corpus.modules).get(key)),
  )

const frontmatterPartLocations = (module: LoomModule, key: string): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.part),
    Option.filter((part) => partKey(bookOf(module), part.value) === key),
    Option.map((part) => ({ path: module.path, position: part.position })),
    Option.toArray,
  )

const partGroup = (modules: Modules, key: string): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  ...Option.toArray(Option.fromNullishOr(partOpenerIndex(modules).get(key))),
  ...Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) => [
    ...frontmatterPartLocations(module, key),
  ]),
]

const partGroupAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.match(partKeyAt(corpus.modules, path, offset), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
    onSome: (key) => partGroup(corpus.modules, key),
  })

const bookLoomsOf = (modules: Modules, book: string): ReadonlyArray<LoomModule> =>
  Array.filter(
    Array.fromIterable(modules.values()),
    (module) => isBookLoom(module) && Option.contains(bookOf(module), book),
  )

const bookLoomLocation = (modules: Modules, book: string): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Array.head(bookLoomsOf(modules, book)),
    Option.flatMap((module) =>
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.book), (line): CorpusLocation => ({
        path: module.path,
        position: line.position,
      })),
    ),
  )

const bookLines = (modules: Modules, book: string): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) =>
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.book),
      Option.filter((line) => line.value === book),
      Option.map((line): CorpusLocation => ({ path: module.path, position: line.position })),
      Option.toArray,
    ),
  )

const bookNameAt = (module: LoomModule, offset: number): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.book),
    Option.filter((line) => covers(line.position, offset)),
    Option.map((line) => line.value),
  )

const bookDefinitionAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((module) => bookNameAt(module, offset)),
    Option.flatMap((book) => bookLoomLocation(corpus.modules, book)),
  )

const namesBook =
  (book: string) =>
  (place: Place): boolean =>
    place._tag === 'Book' && place.book === book

const bookNames = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  book: string,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  ...bookLines(modules, book),
  ...anchorsNaming(catalogue, modules, (step) => namesBook(book)(step.place), spanOf),
]

const bookNamesAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.match(
    Option.flatMap(Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)), (module) =>
      bookNameAt(module, offset),
    ),
    {
      onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
      onSome: (book) => bookNames(catalogueOf(corpus.modules), corpus.modules, book, spanOf),
    },
  )

~

`definitionAt` follows the reference under a cursor to its definition — a section anchor to the heading or chapter title its segment names, a warp anchor to its warp, the name of a chapter box or a discussion box, or a chapter's own title to that chapter, the name of a trope box to the trope's entry point, a `Part R` to where the part is announced, and a `Book:` line to the book loom that opens the book. `referencesAt` returns every place the thing under the cursor is named — a section and its anchors, a warp and its anchors, a chapter's title with the boxes and anchors that name it, a trope's name with the anchors that name the trope, a chapter's number with the anchors that name it by number, a part's announcement and every `Part R` that names it, or a book's `Book:` lines and the anchors whose paths pass through it. Each reads the kind from `symbolAt` and dispatches on it through one `Match`.

=>

export const definitionAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((module) =>
      Option.flatMap(symbolAt(module.doc, offset), (symbol) =>
        Match.value(symbol.kind).pipe(
          Match.when('warpAnchor', () => warpDefLocation(module, path, offset)),
          Match.when('sectionAnchor', () => sectionDefinitionAt(corpus, path, offset)),
          Match.when('boxName', () => boxDefinitionAt(corpus, path, offset)),
          Match.whenOr('frontmatterTitle', 'frontmatterEntry', 'frontmatterRootedIn', () =>
            chapterDefinitionAt(corpus, path, offset),
          ),
          Match.whenOr('frontmatterPart', 'frontmatterPartName', () =>
            partDefinitionAt(corpus, path, offset),
          ),
          Match.when('frontmatterBook', () => bookDefinitionAt(corpus, path, offset)),
          Match.orElse(() => Option.none<CorpusLocation>()),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

export const referencesAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.match(
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
      Option.flatMap((module) =>
        Option.map(symbolAt(module.doc, offset), (symbol) => ({ module, symbol })),
      ),
    ),
    {
      onNone: () => [],
      onSome: ({ module, symbol }) =>
        Match.value(symbol.kind).pipe(
          Match.whenOr('warpAnchor', 'warpDef', () =>
            warpReferences(module, path, offset, (anchor) => anchor.position),
          ),
          Match.whenOr('headingTitle', 'sectionAnchor', () =>
            placeNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, wholeAnchor),
          ),
          Match.when('boxName', () => boxNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, wholeAnchor)),
          Match.whenOr('frontmatterTitle', 'frontmatterEntry', 'frontmatterRootedIn', () =>
            chapterNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, wholeAnchor),
          ),
          Match.when('frontmatterMembership', () => chapterNumbersAt(corpus, path, wholeAnchor)),
          Match.whenOr('frontmatterPart', 'frontmatterPartName', () =>
            partGroupAt(corpus, path, offset),
          ),
          Match.when('frontmatterBook', () => bookNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, wholeAnchor)),
          Match.orElse((): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => []),
        ),
    },
  )

## Renaming a section

A rename is one edit across the scope a name spans, so the name and its references never drift. A section's title moves with every anchor that names the section. A warp's definition moves with every anchor that binds to it. A chapter's declared title moves with every anchor that names the chapter by title, and its number moves with every anchor that names it by number. A book's name moves with every `Book:` line and every anchor that names the book. The exported `renameAt` reads `symbolAt` and gathers these spans through the same functions the references use, with `segmentPosition` as the span. Each span is one name alone: a title, or one segment inside a `::[…]`. So the brackets, the other names of a path, and any `(path)` stay put. A part is not renamed, since its chapters refer to it by a bare number.

=>

export const renameAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.match(
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
      Option.flatMap((module) =>
        Option.map(symbolAt(module.doc, offset), (symbol) => ({ module, symbol })),
      ),
    ),
    {
      onNone: () => [],
      onSome: ({ module, symbol }) =>
        Match.value(symbol.kind).pipe(
          Match.whenOr('warpAnchor', 'warpDef', () =>
            warpReferences(module, path, offset, (anchor) => anchor.name.position),
          ),
          Match.whenOr('headingTitle', 'sectionAnchor', () =>
            placeNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, segmentPosition),
          ),
          Match.when('boxName', () => boxNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, segmentPosition)),
          Match.whenOr('frontmatterTitle', 'frontmatterEntry', 'frontmatterRootedIn', () =>
            chapterNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, segmentPosition),
          ),
          Match.when('frontmatterMembership', () =>
            chapterNumbersAt(corpus, path, segmentPosition),
          ),
          Match.when('frontmatterBook', () => bookNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, segmentPosition)),
          Match.orElse((): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => []),
        ),
    },
  )

~

`navigationRangeAt` is the span a navigable symbol occupies — the whole title, anchor, warp, or `Part R`, read from the same classification the mappings and colours read. It is the range a definition link underlines on hover and the range a rename box covers, so the editor takes the whole name rather than the one word the cursor sits in. The rule is the capability: a kind whose `profileOf` sets `navigation` spans its whole token, and everything that draws a selection reads it here. `renameRangeAt` narrows that to the renameable — the span only where `renameAt` has edits to make — so a part, navigable but not yet renameable, underlines on hover yet opens no rename box.

=>

export const navigationRangeAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((module) => symbolAt(module.doc, offset)),
    Option.filter((symbol) => profileOf(symbol.kind).features.navigation === true),
    Option.map((symbol): CorpusLocation => ({ path, position: symbol.position })),
  )

export const renameRangeAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.filter(
    navigationRangeAt(corpus, path, offset),
    () => renameAt(corpus, path, offset).length > 0,
  )

## Titles beside numbers

A number names a position, not a chapter. When a chapter moves, a numbered reference points at whichever chapter took the number, and it still resolves, so no fault reveals the change. So the editor shows, beside each segment that names a part or a chapter by number, the title it stands for, and a reader sees where the reference points.

`hintsAt` gives those titles for one loom. A `CorpusHint` places a label at an offset in the loom's source, the end of the numbered segment. `labelOf` reads the label a place shows: a chapter's title, or a part's name when the book knows it. A section is never named by number, so it shows none.

=>

export type CorpusHint = { readonly offset: number; readonly label: string }

const labelOf = (place: Place): Option.Option<string> =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) => Option.some(chapter.title)),
    Match.tag('Part', ({ part }) => part.name),
    Match.tag('Book', () => Option.none<string>()),
    Match.tag('Section', () => Option.none<string>()),
    Match.tag('Box', () => Option.none<string>()),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

export const hintsAt = (corpus: LoomCorpusAst, path: Location): ReadonlyArray<CorpusHint> => {
  const catalogue = catalogueOf(corpus.modules)
  return Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusHint> => [],
    onSome: (module) =>
      pipe(
        module.doc.sections,
        Array.flatMap(anchorsOf),
        Array.flatMap((anchor) =>
          pipe(
            stepsOf(catalogue, path, anchor),
            Array.filter((step) => step.numbered),
            Array.map((step) =>
              Option.map(labelOf(step.place), (label) => ({
                offset: segmentPosition(anchor, step.segment).end.offset,
                label,
              })),
            ),
            Array.getSomes,
          ),
        ),
      ),
  })
}

## Braces waiting for a label

An anchor takes a label in braces right after it, as `::[Greeting function]{To be settled}` does. An author types the label one letter at a time, so the editor offers the labels an anchor takes while the cursor stands between the braces. To make that offer, the editor needs the span the chosen label replaces.

The `labelSlotAt` function finds that span in one loom. A `LabelSlot` is the span, the slot a label fills: its `start` and `end` in the loom's source, and whether braces close it. Closed braces form a specifier, and the slot is the label between them. A brace the author has only opened forms nothing yet, because the tokeniser reads an open brace without its close as ordinary text. The slot then runs from just after the brace to the cursor, as long as the cursor stays on the anchor's line. Braces that hold parameters already name their keys, so they hold no slot. The `slotOf` function reads the slot of one anchor, after its target when it has one, and keeps the slot only when the cursor stands inside it.

=>

export type LabelSlot = {
  readonly start: number
  readonly end: number
  readonly closed: boolean
}

const openSlot = (text: string, brace: number, offset: number): Option.Option<LabelSlot> =>
  text[brace] === '{' && brace < offset && !text.slice(brace, offset).includes('\n')
    ? Option.some({ start: brace + 1, end: offset, closed: false })
    : Option.none()

const slotOf =
  (text: string, offset: number) =>
  (anchor: WarpAnchorToken): Option.Option<LabelSlot> =>
    pipe(
      Match.value(anchor.specifier).pipe(
        Match.when({ type: 'Specifier' }, ({ open, close }) =>
          Option.some({
            start: open.position.end.offset,
            end: close.position.start.offset,
            closed: true,
          }),
        ),
        Match.when({ type: 'Parameters' }, () => Option.none<LabelSlot>()),
        Match.when(Match.undefined, () => openSlot(text, anchor.position.end.offset, offset)),
        Match.exhaustive,
      ),
      Option.filter((slot) => slot.start <= offset && offset <= slot.end),
    )

export const labelSlotAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<LabelSlot> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((module) =>
      Array.findFirst(allAnchorsOf(module.doc), slotOf(module.text, offset)),
    ),
  )

## Keeping a box in step

A box that points to a loom holds a copy of the loom's summary. The loom holds the summary's source, and the box holds the copy, so a reader of the section sees what the loom is about without leaving it.

A chapter's summary is its opening prose, the lines of its opening section up to its first code. `openingProseOf` reads it from the lines `openingLinesOf` gives, without the boxes among them, since a box never holds another. `outsideBoxes` drops every box from a run of lines, and `stillInBox` follows whether a line stands in one. `textOf` joins lines into text, and `tidy` evens out what a copy may change without meaning to: the kind of line ending, the blank lines between paragraphs, and the space at either end.

=>

const tidy = (text: string): string =>
  text
    .replace(/\r\n?/g, '\n')
    .replace(/\n{3,}/g, '\n\n')
    .trim()

const textOf = (lines: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): string =>
  tidy(
    Array.join(
      Array.map(lines, (line) => line.source),
      '',
    ),
  )

const stillInBox = (inBox: boolean, box: BoxToken): boolean =>
  Match.value(box.side).pipe(
    Match.when('open', () => true),
    Match.when('close', () => false),
    Match.when('stray', () => inBox),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const outsideBoxes = (lines: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
  Array.getSomes(
    Array.mapAccum(lines, false, (inBox, line): readonly [boolean, Option.Option<LoomWeft>] =>
      Option.match(boxOf(line), {
        onNone: () => [inBox, inBox ? Option.none() : Option.some(line)],
        onSome: (box) => [stillInBox(inBox, box), Option.none()],
      }),
    )[1],
  )

const openingLinesOf = (section: LoomSection): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
  Array.takeWhile([...section.preamble, ...section.code], (line) => line.type !== 'ArrowWeft')

const openingProseOf = (module: LoomModule): string =>
  Option.match(openingSectionOf(module), {
    onNone: () => '',
    onSome: (section) => textOf(outsideBoxes(openingLinesOf(section))),
  })

~

A discussion's summary is its opening prose, followed by the conclusion of each of its topics. Each conclusion becomes a paragraph of plain prose, without its box, in the order of the topics. A discussion's opening runs up to its first message, and `discussionOpeningOf` reads it. `topicsOf` lists the sections a divider opens, and `conclusionOf` reads the body of a topic's conclusion, the first box of the `Conclusion` kind among the topic's lines. `discussionSummaryOf` joins the opening and the conclusions, leaving out any that is empty.

`summaryOf` reads a loom's summary of either kind, and `isDiscussion` tells a discussion by its identity line.

=>

const isDiscussion = (module: LoomModule): boolean =>
  module.doc.frontmatter?.discussion !== undefined

const discussionOpeningOf = (module: LoomModule): string =>
  Option.match(openingSectionOf(module), {
    onNone: () => '',
    onSome: (section) =>
      textOf(
        outsideBoxes(
          Array.takeWhile(
            [...section.preamble, ...section.code],
            (line) => line.type !== 'TildeWeft',
          ),
        ),
      ),
  })

const topicsOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<LoomSection> =>
  Array.filter(module.doc.sections, (section) => section.heading.marker.type === 'Divider')

const conclusionOf = (topic: LoomSection): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    boxesAmong([...topic.preamble, ...topic.code]),
    Array.findFirst((opened) => boxKind(opened.box) === conclusionKind),
    Option.map((opened) => textOf(opened.body)),
  )

const discussionSummaryOf = (module: LoomModule): string =>
  Array.join(
    Array.filter(
      [discussionOpeningOf(module), ...Array.getSomes(Array.map(topicsOf(module), conclusionOf))],
      (paragraph) => paragraph !== '',
    ),
    '\n\n',
  )

const summaryOf = (module: LoomModule): string =>
  isDiscussion(module) ? discussionSummaryOf(module) : openingProseOf(module)

~

A box may point to a loom nobody has written. The editor then offers to write it, and `openingOf` gives the `Opening` it writes: the new loom's `file` and its `text`. The new loom belongs to the box's own book and part, and takes the part's next number of its kind, which `nextNumber` finds, since chapters and discussions are numbered apart. A box in a loom that stands in no part gives the new loom only the box's book, which `noPartIn` holds. A part without numbers names its looms by title alone, so there the new loom takes no number. `membership` writes the identity line in the form the part already uses, and names the new loom a `Chapter` or a `Discussion` in it. The new loom's opening prose is the box's body, so the copy and its source agree from the start.

The two kinds differ in where the new loom stands and what its identity holds besides. `fileOf` places it. A new chapter stands beside the loom of the box, in a file named for its number and its title, which `slugOf` turns into lowercase words joined by hyphens, as `07-rooms.loom`. A new discussion stands in the `discussions` folder beside the loom of the box, which `discussionsFolderOf` finds, in a file named for its title alone, as `discussions/waking.loom`. `fieldsOf` adds the rest of the identity. A new loom names the box's book, when the box stands in a named book, so it joins the same book. A new chapter's status is `Canvas`, as the chapter "Canvases" describes, and it keeps the language of the box's chapter. A discussion holds no code, so it takes neither.

=>

export type Opening = { readonly file: string; readonly text: string }

const noPartIn = (book: Option.Option<string>): PartRef => ({
  book,
  number: Option.none(),
  name: Option.none(),
})

const slugOf = (title: string): string =>
  title
    .toLowerCase()
    .replace(/['’]/g, '')
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, '')

const folderOf = (path: Location): string => path.slice(0, path.lastIndexOf('/') + 1)

const discussionsFolderOf = (path: Location): string => {
  const folder = folderOf(path)
  return folder.endsWith('/discussions/') ? folder : `${folder}discussions/`
}

const numberOf = (chapter: Chapter): number =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(chapter.number, (written) => Number.parseInt(written, 10) || 0),
    () => 0,
  )

const nextNumber = (catalogue: Catalogue, part: PartRef, kind: LoomKind): Option.Option<number> =>
  Option.map(
    part.number,
    () =>
      1 +
      Math.max(
        0,
        ...Array.map(
          chaptersWithin(
            catalogue,
            part.book,
            Option.some(part),
            (chapter) => chapter.discussion === (kind === discussionKind),
          ),
          numberOf,
        ),
      ),
  )

const membership = (
  part: PartRef,
  number: Option.Option<number>,
  title: string,
  kind: LoomKind,
): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  Option.match(Option.all([part.number, number]), {
    onSome: ([numeral, next]) => [`Part ${numeral}, ${kind} ${next}: ${title}`],
    onNone: () => [
      ...Option.toArray(Option.map(part.name, (name) => `Part: ${name}`)),
      `${kind}: ${title}`,
    ],
  })

const fileOf = (ref: BoxRef, kind: LoomKind, number: Option.Option<number>): string =>
  Match.value(kind).pipe(
    Match.when(chapterKind, () => {
      const numbered = Option.match(number, {
        onNone: () => '',
        onSome: (next) => `${String(next).padStart(2, '0')}-`,
      })
      return `${folderOf(ref.module)}${numbered}${slugOf(ref.name.value)}.loom`
    }),
    Match.when(
      discussionKind,
      () => `${discussionsFolderOf(ref.module)}${slugOf(ref.name.value)}.loom`,
    ),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const fieldsOf = (
  kind: LoomKind,
  book: Option.Option<string>,
  own: Option.Option<Chapter>,
): ReadonlyArray<string> => [
  ...Option.toArray(Option.map(book, (name) => `Book: ${name}`)),
  ...Match.value(kind).pipe(
    Match.when(chapterKind, () => [
      'Status: Canvas',
      ...Option.toArray(
        Option.map(
          Option.flatMap(own, (chapter) =>
            Option.fromNullishOr(chapter.module.doc.frontmatter?.language),
          ),
          (written) => `Language: ${written.value}`,
        ),
      ),
    ]),
    Match.when(discussionKind, (): ReadonlyArray<string> => []),
    Match.exhaustive,
  ),
]

const openingOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, ref: BoxRef, kind: LoomKind): Opening => {
  const own = chapterOf(catalogue, ref.module)
  const book = bookAt(catalogue, ref.module)
  const part = Option.match(own, {
    onNone: () => noPartIn(book),
    onSome: (chapter) => chapter.part,
  })
  const number = nextNumber(catalogue, part, kind)
  const title = ref.name.value
  const body = textOf(ref.body)
  return {
    file: fileOf(ref, kind, number),
    text: Array.join(
      [
        '---',
        ...membership(part, number, title, kind),
        ...fieldsOf(kind, book, own),
        '---',
        '',
        `# ${title}`,
        '',
        ...(body === '' ? [] : [body, '']),
      ],
      '\n',
    ),
  }
}

~

`boxNoticesIn` tells the diagnostics what each box of one loom needs, as a `BoxNotice`. A chapter box or a discussion box whose loom is not written is `Unwritten`, with its kind and the opening that writes it. A trope box that points to no single section or chapter is `Unsettled`, with the `target` its `anchors` value names, when it names one. A box whose body no longer matches what it copies is `Stale`. It carries what the box copies from, which `CopiedFrom` names, with that thing's title, the `summary` the body should hold, and the span of the body, from the line after the opener to the start of the closer. A box that never closes has no body to compare, so it earns no notice, beside the fault the document builder already gives it.

A `Copied` is what a box copies, ready to compare: where it comes from, its title, and its summary. `copiedFromPlace` reads it from a trope's entry point, and `sectionOpeningOf` gives a section's opening prose, the lines before its first code with any boxes left out. `staleOf` compares a box's body with what it copies. `loomNoticeOf` gives the notice of a chapter box or a discussion box, `tropeNoticeOf` the notice of a trope box, and `noticeOf` chooses between them by the box's kind.

`refreshedSummaries` brings every stale box of the corpus back in step, the way `loom refresh` does. It rewrites each loom that holds a stale box, replacing each body with its loom's summary, last box first, so each replacement leaves the spans before it where they were.

=>

export type CopiedFrom = 'chapter' | 'discussion' | 'section'

export type BoxNotice = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  Unwritten: { readonly name: BoxNameToken; readonly kind: LoomKind; readonly opening: Opening }
  Unsettled: { readonly name: BoxNameToken; readonly target: Option.Option<string> }
  Stale: {
    readonly name: BoxNameToken
    readonly of: CopiedFrom
    readonly title: string
    readonly summary: string
    readonly body: Position
  }
}>

export const BoxNotice = Data.taggedEnum<BoxNotice>()

type StaleBox = Data.TaggedEnum.Value<BoxNotice, 'Stale'>

const bodySpan = (ref: BoxRef, closer: LoomWeft): Position => ({
  start: { line: ref.opener.position.start.line + 1, offset: ref.opener.position.end.offset },
  end: closer.position.start,
})

type Copied = { readonly of: CopiedFrom; readonly title: string; readonly summary: string }

const sectionOpeningOf = (section: LoomSection): string =>
  textOf(outsideBoxes(openingLinesOf(section)))

const copiedFromChapter = (chapter: Chapter): Copied => ({
  of: chapter.discussion ? 'discussion' : 'chapter',
  title: chapter.title,
  summary: summaryOf(chapter.module),
})

const copiedFromPlace = (place: Place): Option.Option<Copied> =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) =>
      Option.some<Copied>({
        of: 'section',
        title: ref.section.heading.title?.source ?? '',
        summary: sectionOpeningOf(ref.section),
      }),
    ),
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) => Option.some(copiedFromChapter(chapter))),
    Match.orElse(() => Option.none<Copied>()),
  )

const staleOf = (ref: BoxRef, copied: Copied): Option.Option<BoxNotice> =>
  pipe(
    ref.closer,
    Option.filter(() => textOf(ref.body) !== copied.summary),
    Option.map((closer) =>
      BoxNotice.Stale({
        name: ref.name,
        of: copied.of,
        title: copied.title,
        summary: copied.summary === '' ? '' : `${copied.summary}\n`,
        body: bodySpan(ref, closer),
      }),
    ),
  )

const loomNoticeOf = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  ref: BoxRef,
  kind: LoomKind,
): Option.Option<BoxNotice> =>
  Option.match(boxTarget(catalogue, ref), {
    onNone: () =>
      Option.some(
        BoxNotice.Unwritten({ name: ref.name, kind, opening: openingOf(catalogue, ref, kind) }),
      ),
    onSome: (target) => staleOf(ref, copiedFromChapter(target)),
  })

const tropeNoticeOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, ref: BoxRef): Option.Option<BoxNotice> =>
  Option.match(Option.flatMap(tropePlaceOf(catalogue, ref), copiedFromPlace), {
    onNone: () =>
      Option.some(
        BoxNotice.Unsettled({
          name: ref.name,
          target: Option.map(ref.anchors, (value) => value.value),
        }),
      ),
    onSome: (copied) => staleOf(ref, copied),
  })

const noticeOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, ref: BoxRef): Option.Option<BoxNotice> => {
  const kind = ref.kind
  return isLoomKind(kind) ? loomNoticeOf(catalogue, ref, kind) : tropeNoticeOf(catalogue, ref)
}

const noticesOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: Location): ReadonlyArray<BoxNotice> =>
  Array.getSomes(Array.map(boxesIn(catalogue, module), (ref) => noticeOf(catalogue, ref)))

export const boxNoticesIn = (corpus: LoomCorpusAst, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<BoxNotice> =>
  noticesOf(catalogueOf(corpus.modules), module.path)

const withSummaries = (text: string, stale: ReadonlyArray<StaleBox>): string =>
  Array.reduce(
    Array.sort(
      stale,
      Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (box: StaleBox) => -box.body.start.offset),
    ),
    text,
    (written, box) =>
      written.slice(0, box.body.start.offset) + box.summary + written.slice(box.body.end.offset),
  )

export const refreshedSummaries = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
): ReadonlyArray<{ readonly path: Location; readonly text: string }> => {
  const catalogue = catalogueOf(corpus.modules)
  return pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(corpus.modules.values()),
    Array.map((module) => ({
      module,
      stale: Array.filter(noticesOf(catalogue, module.path), BoxNotice.$is('Stale')),
    })),
    Array.filter(({ stale }) => stale.length > 0),
    Array.map(({ module, stale }) => ({
      path: module.path,
      text: withSummaries(module.text, stale),
    })),
  )
}

~

The editor keeps a box and its loom in step as the author types, and `syncedSummaries` works out how. It compares a loom as it was before an edit with the loom as it is now, against the rest of the corpus, and gives the edits that bring the other side along, each a `CorpusEdit`: the loom it changes, the span, and the text written there.

An edit carries across only what was in step before it. A chapter box or a trope box whose body matched what it copies before the edit, and no longer matches it after, was edited by the author, so `boxToTarget` writes the box's new body into the opening it copies. `copyOf` finds that opening: a chapter's opening section, or a trope's entry point, a section or a chapter. A discussion box takes no such edit. Its summary gathers conclusions from the discussion's topics, so an edit to the box has no one place to go, and it stays a warning until its fix copies the summary back. Neither does a box whose copy lives in its own loom, since the editor is writing that loom already. A loom whose summary changed takes every chapter box and discussion box along that held the old summary, through `loomToBoxes`. A trope's entry point whose opening changed takes every trope box along that held the old one, through `tropesToBoxes`, and `openingsOf` finds the entry point's opening before the edit and after it. A box already out of step, such as one behind a chapter that changed on disk, stays a warning, so an edit elsewhere in its loom never writes an old copy over a newer source. A box that never closes has no body to compare and moves nothing.

An opening can take a box's body only when it is prose alone. `openingEditOf` gives the edit to a section's opening: from the first written line of the opening to the end of the last, or, when the opening holds nothing yet, a new paragraph under the section's heading. An opening that holds a box of its own takes no edit, since writing over it would erase that box, so the warning stays for the author to settle. `contentEnd` is where a line's text ends, before its newline.

=>

export type CorpusEdit = { readonly path: Location; readonly at: Position; readonly text: string }

const contentEnd = (line: LoomWeft): Point => ({
  line: line.position.start.line,
  offset: line.position.start.offset + line.source.replace(/\r?\n$/, '').length,
})

const openingEditOf = (
  section: LoomSection,
  body: string,
): Option.Option<{ readonly at: Position; readonly text: string }> => {
  const lines = openingLinesOf(section)
  const written = Array.filter(lines, (line) => line.source.trim() !== '')
  return Array.some(lines, (line) => Option.isSome(boxOf(line)))
    ? Option.none()
    : Option.some(
        Array.match(written, {
          onEmpty: () => ({
            at: { start: section.heading.position.end, end: section.heading.position.end },
            text: body === '' ? '' : `\n${body}\n`,
          }),
          onNonEmpty: (all) => ({
            at: {
              start: Array.headNonEmpty(all).position.start,
              end: contentEnd(Array.lastNonEmpty(all)),
            },
            text: body,
          }),
        }),
      )
}

type Copy = { readonly module: LoomModule; readonly section: LoomSection }

const chapterCopy = (chapter: Chapter): Option.Option<Copy> =>
  Option.map(openingSectionOf(chapter.module), (section) => ({ module: chapter.module, section }))

const copyOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, modules: Modules, ref: BoxRef): Option.Option<Copy> =>
  Match.value(ref.kind).pipe(
    Match.when(chapterKind, () => Option.flatMap(boxTarget(catalogue, ref), chapterCopy)),
    Match.when(discussionKind, () => Option.none<Copy>()),
    Match.when(tropeKind, () =>
      Option.flatMap(tropePlaceOf(catalogue, ref), (place) =>
        Match.value(place).pipe(
          Match.tag('Section', ({ ref: at }) =>
            Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(at.module)), (module) => ({
              module,
              section: at.section,
            })),
          ),
          Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) =>
            chapter.discussion ? Option.none<Copy>() : chapterCopy(chapter),
          ),
          Match.orElse(() => Option.none<Copy>()),
        ),
      ),
    ),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const boxToTarget = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: Modules,
  was: LoomModule,
  ref: BoxRef,
): Option.Option<CorpusEdit> =>
  pipe(
    Option.all({
      copy: Option.filter(
        copyOf(catalogue, modules, ref),
        (copy) => copy.module.path !== ref.module,
      ),
      before: Array.findFirst(
        pointingBoxesOf(was),
        (old) => old.kind === ref.kind && old.name.value === ref.name.value,
      ),
    }),
    Option.filter(({ copy, before }) => {
      const summary = sectionOpeningOf(copy.section)
      return (
        Option.isSome(before.closer) &&
        Option.isSome(ref.closer) &&
        textOf(before.body) === summary &&
        textOf(ref.body) !== summary
      )
    }),
    Option.flatMap(({ copy }) =>
      Option.map(openingEditOf(copy.section, textOf(ref.body)), (edit) => ({
        path: copy.module.path,
        ...edit,
      })),
    ),
  )

const loomToBoxes = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  was: LoomModule,
  now: LoomModule,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusEdit> => {
  const before = summaryOf(was)
  const after = summaryOf(now)
  return before === after
    ? []
    : pipe(
        Option.toArray(chapterOf(catalogue, now.path)),
        Array.flatMap((chapter) => boxesPointingTo(catalogue, chapter)),
        Array.filter((ref) => ref.module !== now.path && textOf(ref.body) === before),
        Array.map((ref) =>
          Option.map(ref.closer, (closer) => ({
            path: ref.module,
            at: bodySpan(ref, closer),
            text: after === '' ? '' : `${after}\n`,
          })),
        ),
        Array.getSomes,
      )
}

const openingsOf = (
  place: Place,
  was: LoomModule,
): Option.Option<{ readonly before: string; readonly after: string }> =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) =>
      Option.map(
        Array.findFirst(
          was.doc.sections,
          (old) => old.heading.title?.source === ref.section.heading.title?.source,
        ),
        (old) => ({ before: sectionOpeningOf(old), after: sectionOpeningOf(ref.section) }),
      ),
    ),
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) =>
      Option.some({ before: summaryOf(was), after: summaryOf(chapter.module) }),
    ),
    Match.orElse(() => Option.none()),
  )

const tropesToBoxes = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  was: LoomModule,
  now: LoomModule,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusEdit> =>
  pipe(
    Array.flatten(Array.fromIterable(catalogue.boxes.values())),
    Array.filter((ref) => ref.kind === tropeKind && ref.module !== now.path),
    Array.flatMap((ref) =>
      pipe(
        tropePlaceOf(catalogue, ref),
        Option.filter((place) => Option.contains(placeModuleOf(place), now.path)),
        Option.flatMap((place) => openingsOf(place, was)),
        Option.filter(({ before, after }) => before !== after && textOf(ref.body) === before),
        Option.flatMap(({ after }) =>
          Option.map(ref.closer, (closer) => ({
            path: ref.module,
            at: bodySpan(ref, closer),
            text: after === '' ? '' : `${after}\n`,
          })),
        ),
        Option.toArray,
      ),
    ),
  )

export const syncedSummaries = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  was: LoomModule,
  now: LoomModule,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusEdit> => {
  const modules = new Map(corpus.modules).set(now.path, now)
  const catalogue = catalogueOf(modules)
  return [
    ...Array.getSomes(
      Array.map(boxesIn(catalogue, now.path), (ref) => boxToTarget(catalogue, modules, was, ref)),
    ),
    ...loomToBoxes(catalogue, was, now),
    ...tropesToBoxes(catalogue, was, now),
  ]
}

## Diagnostics across the corpus

Every node carries a health, and a health holds diagnostics. `diagnosticsIn` is the walk that reads them out. It descends any node, gathers the diagnostics it carries, and skips the `health` field itself, so a node's diagnostics are collected once rather than again at every child. It keeps every severity — error, warning, and informational note alike — because the editor surfaces them all.

`moduleDiagnostics` is the per-file view: one module's diagnostics from both tiers, the grammatical health on its parsed document and the semantic health on its product. The product pass lands each fault it can judge within one loom on the part it builds for that anchor. An ambiguous anchor leaves an empty `Fragment` carrying the fault, and a cross-language anchor leaves a `NameRef` carrying it. An edge that leaves the loom carries no fault here, because the compiler judges it against the whole corpus. So the walk reads the `product.code` alone. A tangle sink's `File` repeats that same `Code`, and reading it too would count every sink's diagnostics twice.

An anchor that reaches beyond its loom is the one anchor no single module can judge. Its edge names a section the product pass never saw, so the compiler judges it against the whole corpus. The compiler adds what it finds to the module's own diagnostics, both for the open `.loom` in the editor and for the tangle.

A chapter's title is no single module's to judge either, because it is a name among the chapters of its part. `namesakesOf` finds the other looms whose chapters share a module's title within its part, with the title they share, and the compiler raises the `DuplicateName` fault on that title. Two chapters stand in one part when `samePart` says their parts are one, which holds only within one book. A chapter that names no part has no siblings to clash with. Chapters with one title in two parts, or in two books, are no clash either, since a path through the part or the book tells them apart.

A book's name is judged across the corpus too. `bookTwinsOf` finds the other book looms that open a book loom's book, and the compiler raises the `DuplicateName` fault on its `Book:` line, since a book takes its title and its preface from one loom alone. `unopenedBookOf` finds a `Book:` line that names a book no book loom opens. The loom still stands in that book, but the name may be a slip, so the compiler warns on it with the `UnopenedBook` fault.

`corpusErrors` is the whole-corpus view the tangler takes, narrowed to errors. It reads each module's diagnostics through `diagnosticsOf`, keeps the error-severity ones, and returns every failing module with them. So the tangler refuses a broken build with one report rather than failing at the first bad file. `diagnosticsOf` defaults to `moduleDiagnostics`, and the compiler passes a reader that adds the cross-file judgement. A warning never blocks a tangle; only an error does.

=>

const diagnosticsIn = (node: unknown): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => {
  if (Array.isArray(node)) return node.flatMap(diagnosticsIn)
  if (node === null || typeof node !== 'object') return []
  const self =
    'health' in node
      ? (node as { health: { diagnostics: ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> } }).health.diagnostics
      : []
  const nested = Object.entries(node).flatMap(([key, value]) =>
    key === 'health' ? [] : diagnosticsIn(value),
  )
  return [...self, ...nested]
}

export const moduleDiagnostics = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [
  ...diagnosticsIn(module.doc),
  ...diagnosticsIn(module.product.code),
]

export const corpusErrors = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  diagnosticsOf: (module: LoomModule) => ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> = moduleDiagnostics,
): ReadonlyArray<{
  readonly path: Location
  readonly diagnostics: ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic>
}> =>
  pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(corpus.modules.values()),
    Array.map((m) => {
      const diagnostics = Array.filter(diagnosticsOf(m), (d) => d.severity === 'error')
      return diagnostics.length === 0 ? Option.none() : Option.some({ path: m.path, diagnostics })
    }),
    Array.getSomes,
  )

export type Namesakes = {
  readonly title: { readonly source: string; readonly position: Position }
  readonly looms: ReadonlyArray<Location>
}

export const namesakesOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule): Option.Option<Namesakes> =>
  pipe(
    Option.all({ chapter: chapterOf(catalogue, module.path), title: chapterTitleToken(module) }),
    Option.flatMap(({ chapter, title }) =>
      pipe(
        catalogue.chapters,
        Array.filter(
          (other) =>
            other.module.path !== module.path &&
            other.title === chapter.title &&
            samePart(chapter.part, other.part),
        ),
        Array.map((other) => other.module.path),
        Option.liftPredicate(Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty),
        Option.map((looms) => ({ title, looms })),
      ),
    ),
  )

export const bookTwinsOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, module: LoomModule): Option.Option<Namesakes> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.book),
    Option.filter(() => isBookLoom(module)),
    Option.flatMap((line) =>
      pipe(
        bookLoomsOf(catalogue.modules, line.value),
        Array.filter((other) => other.path !== module.path),
        Array.map((other) => other.path),
        Option.liftPredicate(Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty),
        Option.map((looms): Namesakes => ({
          title: { source: line.value, position: line.position },
          looms,
        })),
      ),
    ),
  )

export const unopenedBookOf = (
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  module: LoomModule,
): Option.Option<FrontmatterBookToken> =>
  Option.filter(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.book),
    (line) => bookLoomsOf(catalogue.modules, line.value).length === 0,
  )

## What this module draws on

Effect supplies `Schema` for the model, `Array`, `Option`, and `pipe` for the queries, `Data` for the places a lookup walks through, and `Match` for the dispatch on a place and on the kind under a cursor. The module bundles the parse model `LoomDocument` and the product model `ProductSchema`, with `LoomSection`, the unit the corpus walks, the `WarpAnchorToken` an anchor is, the `WarpToken` a value warp is defined by, and `Diagnostic`, `Position`, and `Point`, what a corpus walk gathers, where a diagnostic lands, and where a segment of an anchor's name begins and ends. `symbolAt` and `profileOf` from `LoomSymbol` name the token under a cursor and read its features, so the navigation reads the same table the mappings and colours do. `boxOf`, `boxKind`, and `isPointingKind` find the boxes of a loom that point somewhere, and `PointingKind` names their kinds. `isLoomKind` and `LoomKind` tell a chapter box or a discussion box, and `chapterKind`, `discussionKind`, and `tropeKind` tell each kind apart. A trope box's `anchors` and `provides` values are each a `ParameterValueToken`, each name on a trope root's `Entries:` line is a `FrontmatterEntryToken`, a `Book:` line's value is a `FrontmatterBookToken`, and `conclusionKind` finds the conclusions a discussion's summary gathers. `Order` sorts the stale boxes from last to first.

=>

import { Array, Data, Match, Option, Order, pipe, Schema } from 'effect'
import { type Diagnostic, type Point, type Position } from '#ast/LoomNode'
import {
  boxKind,
  type BoxNameToken,
  type BoxToken,
  chapterKind,
  conclusionKind,
  discussionKind,
  type FrontmatterBookToken,
  type FrontmatterEntryToken,
  type HeadingTitleToken,
  isLoomKind,
  isPointingKind,
  type LoomKind,
  type ParameterValueToken,
  type PointingKind,
  tropeKind,
  type WarpAnchorToken,
  type WarpToken,
} from '#ast/LoomTokens'
import { boxOf, type LoomWeft } from '#ast/Weft'
import { ProductSchema, type Provided } from '#ast/ProductAst'
import { LoomDocumentSchema, type LoomDocument, type LoomSection } from '#ast/LoomAst'
import { profileOf, symbolAt } from '#ast/LoomSymbol'

